## Version handling for L3 (Replay Contract v1 §7.2)

- `SIM_REVISION` 2 → 3 (`src/systems/replay/ruleset.js`). `LAST_RELEASED_SIM_REVISION` is now 3, so the contract
  would also have allowed a `behavior-change-unreleased:` attestation without a bump. The owner asked that old
  recordings fail visibly rather than desync silently, so the revision is bumped.
- New ruleset archived: `sha256:477b227b38f0221439af2fec4b26a30bcb7050ec99c0832e08a023813bfe6e55`
  (`tools/ruleset/current.json`). The source tripwire is attested `sim-revision-bumped`, and `build-ruleset.mjs --check` is clean.
- Manifests recorded under revision 1 resolve to the archived revision-1 ruleset. `resolveVerifier` then refuses them
  as `historical-verifier-required` instead of reporting a `diverged` replay.
- `GHOST_PHYSICS_VERSION` stays 1, because §7.2(3) says it is not bumped further. Legacy ghost tapes keep §5.3 outcomes
  (`consistent-with` / `inconclusive-legacy`, never "verified"). Ghost riders draw from the recorded trace and are unaffected.
- Two #165 review tests (`tests/replay/fingerprintReview.test.js`) pinned the old rebuild. They now pin the repaint
  keeping colliders: the same pad sensor with its cooldown intact, an unchanged `staticOrder`, and fingerprints equal to a
  twin that never repainted.
- Golden canaries (#117): `hazard-transition` (mask 4, crosses a biome boundary) and `bump` were re-recorded on the new
  build, and again on the earlier Team B v2.18.2 candidate tree. Both are exact PASS against the committed goldens (`feel-diff --use-out`). The other five are mask 0 and never repaint.
- Biome gameplay is data and is unchanged. Zone mods come from `getZoneDefs` → `PhysicsTuner`, hazards and pickups from
  `takeChunk`, and collider material is fixed in `TerrainMesh._takeBody`. A repaint only ever changed body identity and order.

## Other observations

1. **Biome-entry repaint (L3), fixed with owner approval.** See "Version handling" below.
2. **BSOD Gym READY can wait up to 300 ms for a music beat** (`gymBeatSuggestion`, `RunScene.update`). **Owner decision
   (2026-11-21): intended and accepted** as a deliberate audio-to-sim path. It is Gym only (unranked, never taped).
3. **`settings.controlLayout` mid-run** changes `normalizePad` (`variableHop`, `noAutoNudge`). **Owner decision
   (2026-11-21): a gameplay setting recorded in tapes (pad bits), not a presentation leak; nothing to fix.**
4. **Cosmetic terrain reads** (minimap, Fx ground tests, hazard drawing) can build generator chunks early.
   `streamTerrain` gates their physical insertion (#59). The `terrainCache` class allows this, and no matrix cell
   showed it.
5. **Guardrail audit (owner comment, 3D readiness).** All camera reads in the sim path were checked. Before #155 only K1 read
   the rendered camera inside `simTick`; after #155 nothing does. `renderFrame`'s hazard, pickup, shard and mesh culling and `KodiakRider`
   use `_view` for visibility only, and the matrix confirms that.

## Mapping to Replay Contract v1 subsystems

| This digest | Contract v1 (`FP_SUBSYSTEMS`) | Note |
|---|---|---|
| `tick`, `timers.runTimeMs` | `clock` | |
| `rng` | `rng` | same field (`physicsScramble.state`) |
| `sled` | `sled` | subset (pose/vel of all parts + controller timers) |
| `world`, `contacts` | `matter` | **stricter**: absolute body ids and world order are hashed here; the contract excludes ids |
| `terrain` | `terrain` | |
| `hazards` | `hazards` | |
| `powerups`, `shards` | `pickups` | |
| `timers` (effects part) | `effects` | |
| `bsod` | `bsod` | |
| `kodiaks` | `kodiak` | includes per-bear RNG state |
| `score` | `score` | |
| `crash` | `recovery` (partly) | |
| `telegraph`, `sfx`, `events`, `crashReport`, `glitch` | not hashed (presentation, section 6.1) | kept as *cue* class here |

Without ids, L1 would still show in the contract's `sled` and `matter.pairs` (lost warm-start impulses), as
`tricks`, `kodiak` and `bsod` show.

## Limitations

- Chromium only (SwiftShader). Firefox and WebKit are not covered here; they belong to #120 / #161.
- Five fixtures of 8-12 s each. There is no Gym and no ghost riders on screen.
- The browser matrix is manual (not wired into CI or `npm test`). Only the Jest pieces gate regressions in CI-less runs.
- Headless audio: `AudioContext` exists, but it is not tied to a real output device's clock.
- Viewport changes go through Phaser's FIT scaling. The camera works in fixed logical units (960x540), so the
  viewport cannot reach `_view`. That is why viewport cells are expected to be isolated.
- The digest is diagnostic. 32-bit FNV per subsystem per tick is fine for honest divergence and not meant for anything adversarial.

## Running it

```sh
npm run build
npx vite preview --port 8411 --strictPort &
GAME_URL=http://localhost:8411/ RESULTS=cells.json node tools/conformance/causal-isolation-probe.mjs
# options: FIXTURES=tricks,bsod  CASES=camera-zoom-2,cadence-144  OUT=matrix.json  PARALLEL=3
#          EXPECT_ISOLATION=1  (exit 1 on: no result, page errors, a stalled or short run, a length mismatch, a causal leak
#                               outside the #115 cases, a missed positive control, or any cue leak)
#          FIXED_DATE=2026-11-21T12:00:00Z  (the wall-clock day the pages see)
#          RESULTS=cells.json  (each finished cell is saved at once, keyed by build; reruns skip finished cells)
#          CHROMIUM_PATH=...  (defaults to the image's headless shell when Playwright's build is missing)
npm test -- tests/determinism
```

A full run is about 95 browser runs, roughly 45 minutes on a loaded 4-way container.

### Integration after Team A Kodiak release (2026-11-22)

Team A's v2.19.0 shipped Kodiak shove mechanics and ruleset revision 2 (`sha256:b5763476…`) before this PR. The pre-release candidate of this PR also used revision 2, but with different physics. In the actual integration, Kodiak shove code and its fingerprint schema are preserved; this PR now publishes **revision 3** with the collider redraw fix, retaining both released historical rulesets. Earlier matrix evidence on the v2.18.1 candidate must not be misrepresented as newly collected on v2.19.0: verification against the final combined source is tracked in the review.# Cross-engine replay conformance matrix (issue #120)

> **Newer separately source-stamped revision-6 evidence (#236 / PR #240):** see [cross-engine-sim6.md](cross-engine-sim6.md). The historical measurements below remain unchanged.

> **Release context (2026-11-22, v2.19.4):** The measurements and nine recorded tapes below were generated against the v2.18.1 codebase (`7d5780f`). They are historical diagnostic results, **not** current v2.19.3 browser parity results. Subsequent shipped releases changed physics/replay behavior: Kodiak nonlethal shoves in v2.19.0 (SIM_REVISION 2), collider repaint isolation in v2.19.1 (SIM_REVISION 3), and session reset fixes in v2.19.3 (#219). In particular the previously observed biome-mask previous-run collider-order leak has been addressed (#160 and #219). The original per-engine Math-ULP differences remain valuable evidence for #121, but require fresh fixtures and a new matrix before any current release parity claim. The generated results block and its original provenance are preserved.

**Status: diagnostic evidence, non-authoritative.** The per-tick digests live in `tools/conformance/cross-engine-page.js`. They are not a replay format and not a canonical hash; they are to be replaced by the Replay Contract fingerprints (#152, #118/#165) when those land. Subsystem names are the Replay Contract v1.0/v1.1 `FP_SUBSYSTEMS` names (unchanged in v1.1); this digest is coarser than the contract's 13-way split (mapping note in the generated section). No game code, tape format or header was changed by this PR.

**Playwright WebKit is not iPhone Safari.** It is a Linux build of WebKit 27.2 with a Mac Safari user agent (its GL renderer string is a masked value, not a real GPU). A pass or fail there says nothing about real Safari or iOS. No real iPhone, Safari, ARM, Node or hardware GPU was available: those columns are **unsupported in this environment**, not "passing".

## What was run

Real built game (`vite build` of the merge with main 7d5780fe (v2.18.1), no src changes, Phaser 3.90 + Matter, `window.__bbGame`), stepped frame by frame with `game.step(t, dt)` (loop asleep, `RunScene.update` held), so display cadence reaches `SimClock` exactly as in play. Only the final WebGL draw call is skipped (`RASTER=1` restores it); all JS paths including `renderFrame`, camera and telegraph run.

* 9 fixtures, recorded **once** in Chromium with the game's own `GhostRecorder` on the merged build (so headers carry `syncLevel` and `dailyRuleSource`, Replay Contract v1.1 / PR #164), committed as `tools/conformance/fixtures/cross-engine/*.tape.json` and replayed through `TapePlayer`.
* 5 engine configs: `chromium-headless-shell` 157 (SwiftShader), `chromium-full-headless` 153 (headless=new), `chromium-shell-defaultgl` 157, Firefox 159 (**headed under Xvfb**: headless Firefox cannot create WebGL here), Playwright WebKit 27.2. Linux x64, 4 vCPU.
* **Digest groups.** *Causal* (decide divergence): `rng`, `timers` (=`clock`), `terrain` structure (chunk count/ends, hazard and pickup counts), `kodiaks`, `bsod`, `sled`, `world` (=`matter`, all bodies), `score` (with trick counters). *Derived, never counted as divergence, own column*: `heightAt/slopeAt` read at the sled x (an `atan2` inside) and the camera-derived telegraph marks (#155).
* **Fresh-context cells** (the headline): every engine x fixture as run #1 of its own fresh browser context at 120 Hz. **Matrix**: 4 cadences (30/60/120/144 Hz) x 2 fresh sessions x 10 runs per session (the 9 fixtures in order, then fixture 1 again) = 400 rows, each compared tick by tick with the reference (fixture as run #1 of a fresh context on `chromium-headless-shell`, 120 Hz), plus the game's own `TapePlayer` checkpoint check (exact sled state every 120 ticks) and the final outcome.
* On a causal divergence, a second pass traces every `Math.*` call per tick in both engines and logs the exact calls at the divergent tick; then both engines are re-run with the differing ops replaced by one engine-independent pure-JS implementation (counterfactual).
* The page clock is pinned to the recording day (`CE_DATE`); `record` also verifies that moving the clock to another day and using a level-1 profile does not change a replay of these tapes (it does not: v1.1 header fields now govern, see finding 5).

Coverage: fifth-run-in-session (recorded as the 5th run of one page, verified in a fresh context), collision-heavy, chunk/sector boundaries with biomes (500 m sector 2, mask 2047), BSOD pursuit and catch (`cause: bsod`), airborne tricks, parachute + air brake, Kodiaks on, Daily rule via a `daily-YYYY-MM-DD` seed (practice). **Not covered:** BSOD Gym (no tape is recorded in the Gym), Draft, upgraded/boosted loadouts, GameOver/retry and pause/resume inside a run, tab-background frames, slow-mo/hit-stop as a targeted case, every biome individually, gamepad/touch, input-edge scheduling at 144 Hz (replays feed per-tick pads, so cadence only exercises `SimClock`).

## Findings (corrected after independent review)

1. **Chromium: 8 of 9 fixtures are stable; the 9th depends on run position.** In fresh contexts all three Chromium configs are identical to the reference in 27/27 cells (digests, TapePlayer, outcome), at every cadence and in both sessions. In the matrix, `chunk-biome-boundaries` as run #4 (after `collision-heavy`) diverges at tick 1: that is finding 4, not an engine difference.
2. **Firefox and WebKit are not bit-identical to Chromium, but the final outcomes mostly are.** Fresh-context, 120 Hz, 18 cells: first *causal* divergence in 13/18 (Firefox 5/9, WebKit 8/9). In the other 5 (Firefox cruise, fifth-run, collision, parachute; WebKit airborne-tricks) only the derived `heightAt/slopeAt` probe differs (a 1-ulp `atan2` difference in a read-only function) and nothing else, no sled delta, no TapePlayer desync. Of the 13 causal cells, the exact TapePlayer checkpoint check desyncs in 10 (the other 3 diverge in the digest between checkpoints but their checkpoints still match). **The final outcome (ticks, crash cause, distance, score) equals the reference in 17/18 cells**; the exception is Firefox `chunk-biome-boundaries` (815 m, no crash, vs 835 m hazard crash). Within an engine the result does not depend on cadence (30/60/120/144 Hz) or session. First causal tick: Firefox 255 to 1698, WebKit 19 to 971 (table below, with the sled delta in ulps). The earlier headline "diverges at tick 554 in terrain" was a probe artifact and is withdrawn.
3. **`Math.*` is the cause, and the counterfactual shows only that the engines then agree.** For identical arguments, `Math.hypot` (WebKit and Firefox), `Math.exp` (Firefox, 1 ulp from tick 1), and intermittently `sin`, `cos`, `tan`, `atan2` return different last bits; most are absorbed, and the state diverges where one feeds a collision or angle. With the differing ops replaced by one pure-JS implementation in **both** engines (installed before the game loads), there is no divergence over the whole run in all 13 diagnosed Firefox/WebKit cells (30 Hz stepping, run #1 of one fresh context, one session each; none of the ops was left unreplaced: `tanh` is now in the shim and no `tanh` difference occurred in this run). Caveats: this is "patched A == patched B", not "restores the recording": the patched sim desyncs from the recorded tape at the first checkpoint (tick 120) in every case, because the recording used native Math. No per-op ablation was done (left to #121). Evidence for #121, not a fix.
4. **Session-position leak: historical pre-fix game bug for biome-mask runs; #219 merged in v2.19.3.** One page, tape A then tape B, B compared with B as run #1 of a fresh context (Chromium shell, 120 Hz): `collision-heavy -> chunk-biome-boundaries` diverges at tick 1 (`matter`: same body set, different world-array order), TapePlayer desyncs at tick 120, 815 m survived instead of 835 m crash. Controls `cruise-baseline -> chunk-biome-boundaries` and `airborne-tricks -> chunk-biome-boundaries` are clean. **Root cause (found in #219): `RunScene.create()` never reset `_biomeSector` / `_lastBiome`. With a biome mask, tick 1 of a fresh page sees a sector change and calls `refreshPalette`, which removes and re-adds every collider and reorders the Matter world; a later run whose previous run ended in the same sector does not. It therefore only affects biome-mask fixtures such as `chunk-biome-boundaries`.** #219 resets those fields (shipped v2.19.3); this diagnostic PR itself does not alter source behavior. The same crash fixture twice in a row also shows a smaller difference in the causal `terrain` structure group (hazard/pickup/chunk counts) in this harness: `airborne-tricks` run #2 at tick 1728, `parachute-airbrake` 968, `kodiaks-on` 2150, `bsod-pursuit-catch` 7003, with sled, checkpoints and final outcome unchanged. #219 could **not** reproduce the airborne-tricks tick-1728 case on current main, either through this harness path or through the real crash -> GameOver -> `retry()` path (513 m both ways), and these fixtures have biome mask 0, so the `_biomeSector` leak cannot explain them; the cause of this structure-count difference is **unexplained** and has no sim or outcome effect that this harness can see. The harness itself force-stops scenes and calls `TitleScene.startRun`; it does not use the real retry flow.
5. **Date and Sync Level (Replay Contract v1.1).** On the pre-merge build the same cruise tape ended at 2401 ticks / 591 m with the clock on 2026-11-21 and desynced at tick 120 / 352 m on 11-22 (today's Daily rule applied, header `dailyRuleId` ignored), and `BsodPursuit` read the live profile level. Main now records `dailyRuleSource` and `syncLevel` in the tape header and replays from them (v1.1 plus PR #164). With the re-recorded tapes, replays on another calendar day and on a level-1 profile are identical to the recording (`fixtures/cross-engine/record-meta.json`: `dateIndependence`, `levelSensitivity`). Tapes recorded before that change still depend on the clock and profile.

## Unavailable or unsupported in this environment

* Real Safari / iPhone / iPadOS, Edge, Chrome for Android, Node, ARM64, Windows, macOS, hardware GPUs.
* Firefox headless (no WebGL here; Phaser is WebGL-only): Firefox ran headed under Xvfb with Mesa software GL.
* Playwright 1.65 expects Chromium build 1254 but 1243 is installed: the harness resolves installed binaries via `executablePath`; nothing was reinstalled.
* GPU rasterisation is skipped by default for speed (16x); `RASTER=1` enables it.

## Reproduce

```
npm run build && npx vite preview --port 8412 &
export GAME_URL=http://localhost:8412/
export CE_FIXTURE_SET=sim6                             # revision-6 definitions, incl. tested Kodiak encounter seed
export CE_TAPE_DIR=/tmp/bsb-ce-sim6-NEW                 # MUST be a NEW, nonexistent directory; choose a new one on every recording
xvfb-run -a node tools/conformance/cross-engine.mjs record               # creates only CE_TAPE_DIR; never overwrites old tapes
xvfb-run -a node tools/conformance/cross-engine.mjs matrix --resume      # 400-row matrix (all engines, 4 cadences, 2 sessions)
xvfb-run -a node tools/conformance/cross-engine.mjs fresh --resume       # fresh-context cells (the headline)
node tools/conformance/cross-engine.mjs positions                        # session-position probe
node tools/conformance/cross-engine-report.mjs                           # regenerate the tables below
# narrow: ENGINES=webkit HZ=120 SESSIONS=1 FIXTURES=cruise-baseline OUT=/tmp/x.json ...
```

**Historical evidence protection (#236):** recording without `CE_TAPE_DIR` now fails *before launching any browser*. The path must be absolute, must not overlap the entire committed `tools/conformance/fixtures/` tree (including historical, SIM6 and symlink aliases), and must name a directory that does not already exist. Recording also requires a clean committed source, matching built `dist/index.html` served from `GAME_URL` and a provable exact build identity. The command atomically creates a fresh directory and uses exclusive-create writes for all tapes and `record-meta.json`; reruns must use another path. `matrix`, `fresh` and `positions` read the supplied `CE_TAPE_DIR` if set, or the immutable historical directory otherwise. `CE_FIXTURE_SET=sim6` requires an explicit path and refuses any tape set without matching source-stamped SIM6 metadata and byte hashes; omit the flag to analyze historical input evidence. Rebuild with `npm run build` before `record`. This recording-path safety foundation now accompanies the separately source-stamped revision-6 fixture set; see [cross-engine-sim6.md](cross-engine-sim6.md) for exact proof and browser limitations. Do not replace old tapes merely to obtain green results.

`--resume` reads `tools/conformance/.cache/*.journal.jsonl` (git-ignored). The journal is keyed by a fingerprint (build index hash, URL, `CE_DATE`, harness code, fixtures and every tape hash); any mismatch discards it and starts fresh, so stale cells are never reused. A session is the unit of replay state, so an interrupted session restarts from its first run.

