**Music Friday / November 19, 2026 · listening prototypes only — NOT merged, deployed, or wired into the game.**

Marvin suggested that we listen to **all three approaches** before picking the most natural four-beat comeback. These are genuine rendered previews made from the original *One More Run* OGG stem pack plus one new editable MIDI drum performance. The files are deliberately short: **5.755 seconds** of the same song and the same musical context.

## 🎧 Listen — one at a time

1. **[A — Let the original drums play naturally](./A_original-natural-drum-stem.mp3)**. The game's actual drum stem continues unmodified while other instruments are gated out. This sounds most faithful to the recording, but depending on where death occurs it may play a fill, a rest, or more than four strikes.
2. **[B — Recorded MIDI drum-performance option](./B_recorded-midi-drum-kit.mp3)**. A newly authored/recorded 160 BPM MIDI note performance plays four accented quarter-note beats with some quiet hi-hat/ghost-note details. It's rendered through a small **original synthetic** kick/snare/hat kit, NOT transcribed from the existing stem, and NOT a live-recorded acoustic drum kit. **[Edit the MIDI take](./B_recorded-midi-drum-performance_160bpm.mid)** in any DAW using your preferred drum kit.
3. **[C — Four distinct hits from the original drums](./C_four-varied-original-drum-hits.mp3)**. Four different ~190-ms onset excerpts are borrowed from the already-owned original drum stem and sequenced at the common test times. Unlike the current game implementation, it does **not** repeat the exact same 140-ms source slice four times.

For the easiest side-by-side playback, download this folder and open **[the local listening comparison page](./index.html)**, which has one audio player per choice.

### Test controls and limits

All three clips have the **same original six-role band prelude and return**, same source playback clock and recording, **160 BPM** test grid (375 milliseconds per beat), **two silent beats**, and the same overall drum RMS level during the 1.5-second count-in. Common context: the original song from **1:01.800** onward, with simulated fatal crash at clip **0.900 seconds**, beat audition from clip **1.855–3.355 seconds**, and full band return at **3.355 seconds**. The 205-ms scratch is **deliberately omitted** in all three to isolate the drum-comeback comparison; Marvin separately found the existing scratch unconvincing, and that sound remains a distinct redesign project.

**Important:** The original Suno prompt explicitly requests 160 BPM, but this audition's 375-ms intervals and chosen source phase are **research approximations**, not a reviewed beat-position grid. Nothing about these clips approves exact OGG onset alignment, the production crash sound, or Gym timing. No original stem files or game soundtrack were modified. The MIDI file describes events, not an acoustic live recording.

The high/low frequencies and attack character of B differ from A/C because B intentionally uses a different synthesized drum kit; that's exactly what the listening test should reveal. Avoid interpreting the digital kit as the only way to render the MIDI—a good sampled live-drum kit could sound very different.

**What to tell Music Friday:** Which of A, B, C feels most like Maxi's band, and whether a hybrid using natural drums with varied fills on missing beats would be better. There's no wrong answer here, and the audition shouldn't change gameplay or music settings until you select a direction.

**Provenance:** original track pack `33_local-one-more-run-bitstream-bluffs` (segment 5, 48 kHz; original stem samples untouched). The comparison renderer is `tools/music/render_three_drums_comparison.py` on this feature branch. Source hashes and sample settings are in [`metadata.json`](./metadata.json).# Music Friday — 'Piano-friendly' melody simplification (fact finding, design ONLY)

**November 19, 2026. Marvin explicitly says a question is NOT a request to implement.** No new audio demo, MIDI render, source edit, score rewrite, game feature, deployment, or public release has been authorized by this discussion. This note documents whether it is possible and an experimental future plan **if separately approved**.

## What Marvin wants to be able to hear someday

One optional, **slightly quantized, more instrument-like rendering** of Maxi's melody. He is fine if it sounds a little more rigid and loses some of a singer's nuance. Preserve clearly *musically meaningful* movement, e.g., a note that rises from low to high across a sustained scream. Remove unrelated little end-of-note pitch scoops that an ordinary piano would not perform if playing the underlying written melody. Do **not** assume all pitch bends are undesirable, all notes need the same vibrato treatment, or that any replacement should overwrite Maxi's original sung performance.

**Three distinct dimensions** need independent controls:

1. **Pitch spelling / stable note centers:** estimate the robust stable pitch center (in semitones) for each note and snap ambiguous cents fluctuations to its musical note. Avoid octave flips and harmonics from raspy screams. A piano-like result is typically flat within each played note, with changes represented by deliberate new discrete notes rather than continuous portamento.
2. **Micro-timing:** optionally pull starts toward the actual song's verified beat/subdivision grid a **little**, not all the way. Respect pickup notes, intentional behind-the-beat lines such as *'three runs ago'*, syncopation and the deliberate half-time bridge. If the beat grid has not been independently verified, prefer the original onset timings and avoid false 'quantization to song time'. A fixed grid at exactly 160.000 BPM has no source-verified phase and is therefore just a prototype timing reference, NOT a production tempo truth.
3. **Expression / bends:** retain structural slides with an audible meaningful destination; suppress vibrato, wobble, tiny ornamental bends, unstable note-tail flicks, and detector artifacts when a pianist would simply hold/release the note. Large meaningful scream glides can become a controlled pitch slide for a flexible synth OR a stepped/legato series of nearby notes for piano. Instrument renders should not pretend a piano pitch-bends if the instrument does not support it.

## Source signal reality: the existing score cannot already do this

`tools/music/build_provisional_melody_v1.py` currently extracts f0 from the original **vocals stem** with `librosa.yin` at 16 kHz / 10 ms hop, rounds notes immediately to integer MIDI semitones, applies a nine-frame median filter, merges stable runs and renders a provisional **harmonic replacement voice**. The separate game `bitstream-performance-v1` `events.melody` schema contains `startFrame`, `endFrame`, integer `pitch`, ID, and source fingerprints. It **contains no time-varying note pitch-bend/portamento envelopes**. In-game Score Inspector is a *read-only diagnostic* score and OGG waveform view, not a verified human MIDI transcription. Its source note candidates are **provisional-unreviewed**. The V2 score validator explicitly requires unchanged melody events unless separately versioned. Simply toggling a nonexistent `pitchBend=false` field or rounding the current integer MIDI score again would produce no meaningful new interpretation.

**To hear the requested alternative later** we must work *offline* from the original **source-bound vocals audio**, preserve a 10–20 ms pitch/time contour and voicedness/confidence, then decide note boundaries and optional ornaments before converting to a new separate performance-score artifact and rendering through a chosen instrument. Do **not** change the released performance score, its hash, the actual source OGG, or any other game's consumers. Respect the existing song/stem SHA-256 identity, 48 kHz source frame clock, track segment boundaries and source duration. Store uncertainty as uncertainty (especially screaming/noise/harmonics), not as false note confidence.

## Concrete algorithm to prototype only with future approval

**A. Stable note estimation**

- Extract vocal pitch/voicedness with more than a single method or at least corroborated harmonic salience; source whispers/screams are not reliably monophonic voiced signals. Identify unvoiced consonants, harmonized/chopped hooks, multi-singer sections, and high distortion; never create fake pitch bends from silence/harmonic octave errors.
- Segment by **sustained pitch center, energy and re-attacks**, not by every 10 ms vibrato rise. Compute a robust median for central voiced frames (e.g. central 40–70% of each note); map to nearest stable semitone with optional key-aware constraints only when a key is independently established.
- Preserve real repeated notes at the same pitch and rests; don't merge them merely because pitch centers match. Explicitly retain source offsets and segment IDs.

**B. Classify each note's vocal contour by intent**

- `steady` → replace tiny drift/vibrato with one integer note held to the detected release.
- `onset-slide-to-held-target` → keep only when a sufficiently long, coherent movement arrives at a clear stable destination; allow strong scream climb and opening scoop when it is clearly integral to the melodic contour. On piano, represent deliberate multi-semitone movement as short *discrete* lead-in notes, not a literal piano bend.
- `terminal-scoop-without-next-target` → remove little final upward/downward flourish and sustain/release the stable musical pitch instead. A brief end lift that intentionally leads into the next pitched note is **not** automatically removable.
- `vibrato/unstable/unvoiced` → suppress ornamental fluctuations; retain stable note center and source onset/offset as far as detectable. If octave or pitch is truly ambiguous, flag for human review rather than inventing a confident note.

**C. Adjustable simplification, no magic single switch**

Initial *test-only* parameter hypotheses, not pre-approved values: quantization strength **0.20–0.40** for source-verified onset shifts, bound shift e.g. **≤25 ms**, favor note release preserving phrase lengths; pitch center integer semitone for a piano candidate; optional threshold ~**1–2 semitones** for retaining strong intentional target changes; distinguish tail flourishes with a tentative ~**80–150 ms** tail window and *whether a next stable pitch is reached*. These thresholds should be optimized against manually curated windows, not frozen because they sound mathematically tidy. Half-time bridge and behind-beat lyric phrasing require **different musical priors** from hard-attack chip riffs.

**D. Future controlled listening comparison**

- `A` **original vocal/song** or current source melody surrogate: reference, with no remapping.
- `B` **piano-friendly, lightly quantized**: piano/clean chiptune lead playing the *interpreted melody*, full-word ornaments omitted, big deliberate motion represented by meaningful neighboring notes. This is the option Marvin specifically asked whether he could eventually hear.
- `C` **moderately expressive**: same notes/onsets and instrument/loudness but retains only the clearly identified phrase-defining slides (or equivalent discrete piano notes). Compare to B with as few uncontrolled differences as possible.

All demos should use the **identical original accompaniment, 160 BPM style provenance, same time window, same player transport and source-tracked hashes**. The original Suno prompt requests 160 BPM and a half-time bridge, but recording timing and phase remain unverified; audition on an energetic chorus, a genuine scream/glide, a terminal scoop example and the half-time bridge before judging an algorithm. Allow listener to mark *keep glide / drop bend / wrong pitch / wrong note timing* per phrase; never promote speculative changes to a verified score without reviewed examples.

## Invariants and human approval gate

- Never alter or overwrite original vocals, original six stems, released performance score/OGG, player saved profile, or verified BeatGrid catalog.
- An optional future rendering is a separate versioned, source-hash-bound experimental score **with a distinctly named contour/simplification schema**. Do not silently insert bends into `bitstream-performance-v1` or violate the existing V2 melody-unchanged rule.
- Unit tests must cover short terminal scoop vs genuine pitch transition, stable screamed glide, vibrato suppression, unvoiced/noisy frames and octave errors, plus preserved re-attacks/pickups/half-time. Include human listening before any feature selection.
- **No audio was generated in response to this question.** Generating B/C, implementing a quantization control, or shipping it to Music Lab would require a later explicit go-ahead. This is capability/design exploration only.# Music Friday — More convincing vinyl and four-beat crash comeback

**Design research, November 19, 2026. No game code, samples, score catalog, or deployment altered.**

## What Marvin has already decided, versus what remains open

Marvin approved **fatal crash → short vinyl scratch → 1–3 beats' silence → four beats of drums → original full band**, with the soundtrack's transport still moving and instant retry never held up. The existing effect is merged and released. On actual listening Marvin said **the comeback sounds great**, but the four identical sampled drum hits sound *somewhat looped* and the noise/triangle-tone scratches do *not* sound like real record scratching. He suggested, as alternatives rather than a final choice, **the original drum stem**, **a newly recorded MIDI drum performance** (not extracting MIDI from the original), and **several different original source drum hits**. Marvin then approved **auditioning all three**. Their short playable A/B/C previews and editable MIDI are published separately at `docs/music-review/drum-countin-ab-20261119/` on this feature branch. No winner has been chosen.

## The root cause of today's scratch timbre

`src/systems/music/TurntableScratch.js` currently generates a 205 ms filtered-noise sweep, playback-rate envelope and separate triangle oscillator. It has **no recorded musical PCM moving backwards or forwards**. Spectral movement is not enough: a convincing turntable scratch generally needs audible music transients sliding in pitch/time, non-linear hand motion and abrupt crossfader cuts. Passing existing scratch duration, direction, mute and cancel tests does **not** show that it sounds like a physical record. The current manual Pause/Resume implementation must remain separately tokenized and exact-frame-resumable; fatal scratch must never introduce a second playlist player or restart the song.

### Proposed design S1: platter scrub of the already licensed song

- **Precompute on track readiness, not during crash:** take a bounded stereo snippet, e.g. 160–350 ms of rhythmically identifiable guitar/chiptune/drum mixture around a suitable onset from the *already decoded current OGG* source. Record the source segment/frames and SHA-256; don't encode or duplicate full music masters. Generate a small reversed view/copy of that snippet using existing PCM samples so backward playback is reliable across browsers. No network SFX dependency. This is a **scratch effect one-shot**, not a second song transport.
- **Define a physically motivated gesture, not an abstract oscillator sweep.** An initial plausible 205 ms stop-scratch envelope: rapid short forward drag, brief backward pull, then a decelerating forward stop; resume is a compatible forward re-acceleration. Piecewise playback position is integrated from a continuously varying signed velocity with a *short stationary hand pause* and intentional crossfader gaps. Stereo phase and sample continuity are checked at reversals. The absolute song transport never seeks or stops except when the existing manual pause contract expressly freezes it.
- **Audio implementation options:** pre-render a tiny ~205 ms PCM gesture from this snippet for deterministic replay, using band-limited interpolation and fades; OR use several independently scheduled tiny forward/reversed buffer slices on the existing music bus. Do **not** rely on setting negative `AudioBufferSourceNode.playbackRate` at runtime as an untested universal backwards-playback primitive. Pre-rendering tiny buffers is the simpler reproducible research candidate.
- **Crossfader:** 3–8 ms fades at each reversal/cut, a little carefully filtered surface texture, no exposed click/pop, no automatic reverb tail that masks the beat silence. Preserve exact scratch-to-silence handoff. Keep speed and transients audible but avoid over-bright or excessively loud sweeps. Use existing music and master gain controls; muted soundtrack means muted scratch.
- **Available fallback:** if valid current-track samples are absent, maintain existing short synthetic scratch or no scratch, never block a fatal crash, Game Over, initial menu, immediate retry, or manual pause. Ignore stale-generation scratch nodes on track/jukebox/transport changes. No sampler/timbre cache should survive track replacement incorrectly.
- **Optional comparison S2:** a genuinely recorded turntable scratch with clearly verified public-domain/CC0 or licensed provenance (not merely 'free download'). Only adopt it after confirming license and testing whether its sonic character suits the actual music. No external SFX has been obtained or selected yet.

**Reference A/B test (not yet rendered):** S0 existing 205 ms noise/triangle, S1 resampled original-song scrub, and S2 licensed real vinyl scratch *if appropriate*. Keep same original underlying song, same duration, same loudness target and effect route. Test pause and resume separately; a satisfying crash scratch should not force identical gestures on user-initiated Pause and Resume.

## Drum count-in: design to retain source authenticity and four clear beats

Current `FatalCrashCountIn.findOriginalDrumAccent()` chooses one audible ~140 ms source hit then plays **that identical PCM clip four times**. This mechanically guarantees four audible beats at unverified 0.5s spacing, but explains the audible repetition Marvin noticed. The current game has a distinct `recovery` gain on every source in `MusicFXGraph`, which can temporarily route a natural original drum stem while gating other stems. That source gate works without altering adaptive/orchestra motion gains or the original song transport. Existing MIDI demo is an editable SMF0 with a new original synthetic kit; it is **not** transcription or an acoustic performance.

**Next candidate D — original-drum-anchored adaptive hybrid (recommendation, not approval):**

1. Identify the actual recording's quarter-note phase before switching from synthetic research timing. The original *One More Run* Suno style prompt specifies **160 BPM** and deliberately includes a **half-time bridge**. A strict 375 ms clock is a **test configuration**, not the approved song's true quarter-note frame grid, and cannot be inserted into `RELEASE_BEAT_GRIDS` without verification.
2. When fatal crash occurs, keep original `StemMusicDirector` clock and all source nodes moving. Gate instruments/voice away. At the start of the four-count, allow the original **`drums` source input** at normal user mix gain to pass while all other sources remain gated, *if* that passage contains suitably clear original strikes. If a true natural rest/fill leaves a weak beat, layer one **different** source-derived hit on that scheduled slot; don't mindlessly fire all four extra accents over already audible natural hits.
3. Define **four musical count slots**, not merely four sampled source nodes. For each count slot the algorithm must guarantee a perceivable accent if the user requires exactly four, but natural hi-hat/ghosts should not be mistaken for primary kick/snare beats. Use short attack windows and minimum-perceived-level safeguards. On active source hits, use zero or subtle reinforcement, avoiding obvious flams. In a breakdown or drumless intro, switch to arranged varied original excerpts or a previously approved MIDI-rendered drum kit.
4. At the next phase-verified downbeat **after four count pulses**, restore all original music channels with a short click-free ramp. Preserve the source song's position and all prior mute/jukebox choices. A new immediate run does not reset this plan. Manual Pause, explicit jukebox selection, soundtrack mute/disable and ordinary quit retain their existing authority.
5. If beat grid is **not** verified for a particular track/section, keep using the **current synthetic, explicitly unverified and gameplay-safe behavior**. Do not claim an unverified inferred beat is source truth or block rider physics. No extra stem decoders/player, repeated Git assets, new full OGG copy, or memory-unsafe late buffering.

### Engineering validation required before choosing/integrating a sound

- **Musical listening**: A natural original drums, B recorded-MIDI drum kit, C four distinct original hit slices; possibly D hybrid. Audition all four songs, multiple source sections including drumless intros, fills, transitions and the *One More Run* half-time bridge. Distinguish 'sounds musically intentional' from mere 'four hits audible'.
- **Sound/quality**: match LUFS or short-window RMS for *comparison* (never independently master the different versions into incomparable mixes); check peaks/headroom, clicks at source cuts, choppy reverse sound, phase continuity, and 205ms silence boundaries. Human chooses feel before shipping.
- **Safety**: real fatal BSOD vs shield save and Gym catch; crash sound unaffected by RunEnd, four count slots survive immediate retry/new run ID; no frozen song clock; optional audio/codec failures cannot trap Game Over. Correct behavior for both a source rest and four naturally audible drums. Unit tests and real four-track Chromium WebAudio waveform and gameplay tests. Music mute, reduced volume, and user jukebox intent override every one-shot and envelope.
- **Documentation/provenance**: mark source SHA + chosen excerpt offsets, extracted/recorded MIDI provenance, intended 160 BPM vs measured pulse, human listening approval, and original-vs-hybrid choice. Log synthetic research grids as synthetic, never silently promote them to source-approved beat catalogs.

**Release gates remain separate:** this is *design only*. Original vocal performance and sampled original songs stay unmodified. No need to change World Dance or its visual-only energy reaction to music during these sound experiments.# Cosmetic-only synthetic beat and downbeat — API handoff

**November 19, 2026 — Music Friday.** Owner requested adapting the **already-existing artificial 0.5-second crash count-in beat** to expose the **start of fake 4/4 measures**, merge that safe additive interface to `main`, then investigate genuine source downbeat detection separately. This work is the synthetic stage; **no one has verified the true bar-one position in any original song.**

## What the existing fake pulse actually was

`src/systems/music/FatalCrashCountIn.js` previously used **0.5 seconds per artificial count-in beat**: two silent slots followed by four drum accents cut from an original OGG stem. It was **not a whole-song beat API**. `AudioSynth.getBeatTiming()` continues to require a trusted, independently human-verified source grid and fails closed for every released song. World Dance currently reacts to actual OGG energy, not to this fake clock.

## New additive read-only API

`AudioSynth.getSyntheticMeterTiming()` returns a frozen source-clock snapshot when an original track is actively running and audible, otherwise `null`. It assumes **half-second beats**, **four per measure**, fake measure 0 beginning at **original recording source time 0.000 seconds**. These are arbitrary musical assumptions, not inferred downbeats or an endorsed tempo/time signature.

Example at actual song position **2.03 seconds**, assuming WebAudio `clockStart=10.000`:

```js
{
  trusted: false,
  timingKind: 'synthetic-unverified',
  use: 'cosmetic-only',
  canAuthorizeGameplay: false,
  assumedMeter: '4/4-synthetic',
  beatSeconds: 0.5,
  beatsPerMeasure: 4,
  measureSeconds: 2,
  sourceTimeSeconds: 2.03,
  beatIndex: 4,             // zero-based, globally within this original song
  beatInMeasure: 1,         // one-based: 1, 2, 3, 4
  measureIndex: 1,          // zero-based, starting at song t=0
  isMeasureStartBeat: true, // true throughout the first artificial beat
  atMeasureStart: true,     // only near the first 50ms after a bar boundary
  beatStartSeconds: 2,
  measureStartSeconds: 2,
  nextBeatSeconds: 2.5,
  nextMeasureStartSeconds: 4,
  beatStartAudioTime: 12,
  measureStartAudioTime: 12,
  nextBeatAudioTime: 12.5,
  nextMeasureStartAudioTime: 14,
  trackId: '<current original track ID>',
  transportRevision: '<_token>:<track ID>:<clockStart>',
  audioContextTime: 12.03,
}
```

Read each animation tick if needed; **trigger exactly once when `(transportRevision, measureIndex)` changes**, and react to `(transportRevision, beatIndex)` to detect artificial quarter-note pulses. `isMeasureStartBeat` describes which beat you're in, not a one-frame event; do not fire repeatedly while it stays true. In a shorter visual treatment, `atMeasureStart` is a convenience window, not a scheduling guarantee. The WebAudio clock tracks the actual original recording and survives transport bookkeeping; it isn't another player or JS timer. When the jukebox seeks/switches tracks, the `transportRevision` changes and consumers must reset their event guard.

The new access method fails closed to `null` on unavailable/unstarted/paused, muted, disabled, loading/unsupported, manual vinyl pause/resume, fatal crash count-in, non-run menu mode, beyond-source duration and destroyed contexts. It **must not** be used for scoring, physics, Gym release, collision, jumps, achievements, beat-qualified rewards or official leaderboards. Its meter identity and zero source-confidence explicitly prevent accidental trust promotion.

The fatal-crash planner additionally exposes `beatInMeasure:[1,2,3,4]`, `measureStartOnsets:[drumsStart]`, a four-beat measure duration, and `syntheticMeterOrigin:'count-in-first-drum'`. **That crash-only artificial bar origin is not aligned with the song-wide t=0 artificial origin.** Existing scratch, silence, four drum sounds and band-return times did not change.

## Who uses it and what isn't shipped

This commit exposes the information; it **does not modify World Dance** or automatically animate on these fake beats. Control Friday may opt into its own purely visual beat-one emphasis, gated by the already-existing World Dance and Reduce Motion controls. Avoid simultaneous animation triggers from both OGG energy and synthetic bar flags without a clear blend decision. Do not add a separate audio decoder, music timer or gain node.

**Future, separate research:** detect true quarter-note pulse phase, downbeat/measure markers, meter changes, phrase starts, and section boundaries from actual original recorded stems. Output candidate source-bound timestamp maps tagged `unverified` and include manual review. Do not overwrite `RELEASE_BEAT_GRIDS` or rebrand synthetic source t=0 bars as actual song bar one. Music Friday owns that research and will coordinate handoff after this safe synthetic API is merged.

## Automated gate

New `tests/src/music-synthetic-meter.test.js` exercises half-second/4-beat boundaries, measure 1 start and following bars, floating boundaries, arbitrary transport revisions and seek clock changes, missing/paused/muted/fatal/disabled source, invalid meter math, and prevention of verified Gym release. Existing `tests/src/music-gym-beat-timing.test.js`, `tests/src/fatal-crash-countin.test.js` continue to pass. Source contract documented at `src/ARCHITECTURE.md`. No source OGG, performance scores, original beat-grid catalog, scene/physics, saved settings, music graph or deployed visuals are modified by this addition.# Optional piano-friendly *One More Run* melody — first source-grounded listening prototype

**Music Friday · November 19, 2026 · [Issue #37](https://github.com/truevox/BitstreamBluffs/issues/37) · feature branch `feature/music-piano-friendly-melody-20261119`.**

**What this is:** A first, playable **machine-generated, unverified** arrangement of Maxi M.'s sung melody, against *the actual song's original instrument stems*. This is deliberately **an audition, not a replacement** for her finished music. Nothing modifies original vocals, immutable music packs, performance scores, beat grids, saved user settings, or ordinary game audio. It is not a verified or human-corrected music transcription.

## How to listen

From **Settings → Manage Sound → Music Lab → PIANO**, a separate music-review page opens in another browser tab. The isolated Lab music director pauses before opening it. No preview autoplays; choose a passage and listen to its three alternatives. Switching A/B/C **within that passage** jumps to approximately the same playhead time to isolate the melody changes. The preview page is also directly available at `/music-review/piano-friendly/index.html` after Vite/Netlify serves the feature branch. The actual game source soundtrack and user profile are untouched. The player uses the browser's own audio controls, not an in-game persistent music setting; the game does not change volume, mute, gameplay timing or unlock state.

Four 24-second source windows **0:24–0:48, 1:00–1:24, 1:48–2:12, 2:24–2:48**, each with three original-accompaniment context variants:

- **A — Original recorded Maxi vocals:** exact recorded original vocal OGG in this passage over the original drums/bass/guitar/piano/other OGG.
- **B — Piano-friendly simplified melody:** source vocals analyzed by original-OGG YIN/voicedness, stable pitch centers rounded to semitones, tiny vibrato and unmusical note-tail bends removed, represented by a soft **synthetic piano-style instrument**. This can sound stiffer than Maxi's original; that's intentional as a comparison. No verified beat phase exists, so preserve the original detected note onset/offsets rather than forcing grid snap.
- **C — Selectively expressive piano-style melody:** same synthesized instrument and original accompaniment, but the classifier tries to preserve *large coherent onset glides* by rendering short discrete piano stepping notes on the way to the intended held pitch. This is not physical pitch-bending on a piano.

The B/C full-song research scores are also exported as **editable standard MIDI files** (160 BPM tempo from the owner's Suno prompt to encode source-time seconds; this is **not** proof of a verified beat grid). Each note's extracted start, stop, intended pitch, bend label, and input-source 15-segment hash are in `unverified-source-contour.json`; `manifest.json` contains original pack SHA and per-MP3 content hashes. 12 MP3 preview files and the offline self-contained playback HTML are under `public/music-review/piano-friendly/` (Vite serves this at `/music-review/piano-friendly/`). The single offline reconstruction script is `tools/music/render_piano_friendly_melody.py` and can regenerate outputs from a full, licensed OGG pack with cached ffmpeg, librosa, NumPy and SciPy. None of the original source WAV/OGG files are stored again in this feature branch.

## Known limitations / honest interpretation

- The existing `bitstream-performance-v1` schema has only integer note pitches, **not vocal pitch-bend curves**. We extracted the *source vocals themselves*, using librosa YIN pitch at **16kHz/10ms hop**, amplitude RMS and spectral-flatness gating, median filtering, phrase segmentation, and separate stable/structural-glide/terminal-ornament classification. Do not treat this as human-truth piano sheet music. Whispered/screamed/noisy notes, double-tracked vocals, short ornament notes and octave errors can fool a monophonic detector.
- The Suno Style Prompt asks for **160 BPM with a half-time bridge**, but the output recording's beats and phase are **not yet verified**. Timing quantization cannot meaningfully move notes toward an untrusted grid. This initial 'piano-friendly quantized' version therefore **quantizes pitch/ornaments, not song timing**. This is a deliberate fail-safe; actual slight timing quantization remains a later human-reviewed refinement.
- **Passage 2 (1:00–1:24) contains no confidently detected large onset glide.** Its B and C MP3s are identical by design, so this section mainly tests the simplified note interpretation. The page clearly labels that fact.
- The candidate uses a **deliberately synthetic struck-string timbre**, not a sampled Steinway, and its envelopes are simpler than a real pianist's finger/hammer/decay interaction. Repeated phrases with same source pitch can legitimately retrigger. A 'selective expressive' track may be very similar to the simplified track except at candidate strong glides; that is expected.
- Each 24-second passage includes **the original instrument stems and only one voice**, not original lead vocals plus piano together. No singing is replaced in the game's active tracks. Review clip loudness shares common gain within each A/B/C passage, rather than mastering away natural relative differences.
- 4×24 seconds are sampled from the original 174.6s recording for human review. The MIDI/contour analysis spans the full track but a full-length published piano playback track is not claimed.
- Provisional generator counts are candidates, not validated notes; to build trusted note mappings, obtain human markups on meaningful slides, little terminal scoops, unreliable screams and timing against the source recording.

## Acceptance / release policy

Python note-classifier tests include steady note, dramatic rising scream glide, terminal-scoop removal, vibrato, two same-note attacks separated by a rest, unvoiced area, octave spike, source onset preservation, MIDI events and original score immutability. Browser/HTTP/MP3 source assets should be verified before PR review. The current Music Lab button opens an external same-origin listening tab; the page's audio does not share WebAudio user mixer, so **the review page should never auto-play**, and all audio stops on pagehide. Closing the game Lab stops its sandbox and restores normal game music bus as before. The PR should remain **unmerged until owner audition/approval**. In particular, don't infer approval of these note timings, piano timbre, or future replacement from an automated test pass.# Music Friday — Listening room and design notes

These files are for **audition and research**, not new in-game sound settings. The public Bitstream Bluffs game continues to use the previously integrated soundtrack, vinyl effect and original four-hit crash recovery. Nothing in this folder changes game audio or source asset packs.

