- [Piano-friendly, lightly quantized melody design](20261119-piano-friendly-quantized-melody-design.md): an optional future interpretation of Maxi's melody with simplified pitch ornamentation and slight timing quantization. Preserve meaningful vocal/scream glides, remove incidental note-tail scoops. **Historical design-only checkpoint. Issue #37 later approved implementation of a separate provisional A/B/C listening prototype; the original specs remain as an earlier decision.**

## Access and safeguards

These links work directly from the GitHub **`main`** repository once the documentation-only PR has merged. GitHub renders Markdown, but typically does not execute a committed `index.html` as an interactive webpage; use the ZIP for the offline three-player listening room. The code reproducing the previews is at [`tools/music/render_three_drums_comparison.py`](../../tools/music/render_three_drums_comparison.py) and needs full original OGG assets plus its declared offline dependencies to execute.

**No `src/`, production music files, scores, beat catalog, runtime settings, Netlify application files, or gameplay code are modified** by this review package. Publishing these assets to `main` does not make the future quantized melody an active or selectable soundtrack. Owner listening approval is still required before any sound replacement.# #172 / #180 — Mono Lite/Full stem encode audition

**Status: experimental asset-processing helper only, not a released music tier.**

Marvin's requirement: retain every original song performance as an independently addressable stem, create **mono** derivatives so the game can pan each instrument and voice itself (#180), and support a very small Lite download before a better-quality download (#172). Bitrates should vary by instrument; not all stems need the same rate. Preserve original source files and metadata; do not invent synthesized replacements or premix stems.

## Current prototype

`tools/pwa/mono-stem-variants.mjs` provides pure URL/category/bitrate helpers and a source-safe offline encoder. `tools/pwa/encode-mono-sample.mjs` provides a developer-only CLI. `tests/src/pwa-mono-stem-variants.test.js` checks source identification, bitrate choices, URL boundaries, monaural encoder arguments and disjoint input/output roots.

- **One source stem → one mono Opus source derivative** in a separate `/music-variants/lite/packs/...` or `/music-variants/full/packs/...` namespace. The engine-facing logical source remains `/music/packs/...` so the same `StemMusicDirector` can later consume either selected quality, after the downloader/worker is integrated. Audio is generated at 48 kHz and checked for mono channels and matching source duration. Original source has its own SHA-256, each derivative has its own SHA-256, and a per-encode manifest has a version fingerprint.
- Original stereo Oggs are **read-only**; new assets are written outside their tree, checked against filesystem aliasing/symlink escaping. Source and target roots may not overlap. **The encoder refuses every preexisting derivative path** (not just symlinks), and FFmpeg is invoked with `-n`, never `-y`; this protects original stereo masters even if an output was accidentally hardlinked to an original inode or a destination appeared between precheck and write. Repeat encodes require a new separate output directory. The CLI emits no song catalog edits and is not imported by live game code. Do **not** copy generated Oggs into the repository without the correct content review and manifest policy.
- **Per-stem presets are first listening candidates, not final settings.** Lite: bass 12 kbps; lead 18; guitar/piano 20; vocals 24; drums 28; Other 26; adaptive 24. Full mono candidate: bass 40; lead 40; guitar/piano/vocals 48; drums 56; Other/adaptive 52. FFmpeg/libopus uses VBR internally. The caller can override individual source URLs within 6–128 kbps when experimenting with complexity analysis; this is *not* dynamic network bitrate adaptation yet.
- **Mono is a conscious soundstage policy**, not a claim of lossless conversion. Source stems are mostly stereo in the current packs. Downmix can remove stereo-specific differences and force additional lossy re-encoding; compare the original recordings and summed final mix by ear before approving any preset.

## Measured real-source sample (CT223, 2026-11-21)

Generated 24 original recorded stems from *One More Run*, spanning segments `0000`, `0006`, and `0012`. Each set contains vocals, drums, bass, guitar, piano, Other, home voice and Debutmont melody. No source track was copied into source directories or changed.

| Tier candidate | Original bytes | Encoded bytes | Result |
|---|---:|---:|---|
| 24 source stereo Opus files | 2.030 MiB | — | baseline |
| Lite mono (per-instrument rates) | 2.030 MiB | 0.777 MiB | **38.3%** of current source size |
| Full mono candidate (per-instrument rates) | 2.030 MiB | 1.630 MiB | **80.3%** of current source size |

`ffprobe` verified **all 48 derivatives** are mono and 48 kHz. For every segment, reported encoded and source durations matched to the precision measured (max delta `0.00000` seconds; each original file includes an Opus duration offset). No bitrate was determined by an automated perceptual metric. Additional full-catalog measurement and a headphone listening panel are needed before we extrapolate downloads or claim acceptable quality. The **first high-rate preset inflated the eight sampled originals to 142%**, so it was discarded in favor of the lower provisional Full rates. That is a valuable reminder that source audio is already Opus-compressed; mono is not automatically half the bytes.

**Actual generated test files are held in ephemeral `/tmp/bsb-172-mono-24stem-audition-20261121`, not tracked as game assets.** Their manifest hashes are generated from the authentic original recording bytes and derivative bytes.

## Reproduction

```bash
node tools/pwa/encode-mono-sample.mjs lite /path/to/full-song/public /tmp/bsb-mono-sample \
  /music/packs/33_local-one-more-run-bitstream-bluffs/0000-bass.ogg \
  /music/packs/33_local-one-more-run-bitstream-bluffs/0000-drums.ogg
npm test -- --runInBand tests/src/pwa-mono-stem-variants.test.js
```

Use `full` for the higher-rate candidate. A real full-media source checkout or already approved build asset source is needed; sparse game worktrees typically do not contain Ogg bytes. This tool intentionally does not download external audio.

## What is still blocked / not implemented

1. **Perceptual approval:** ABX headphone + phone-speaker comparison for full original vs mono Full vs mono Lite, after summing all active stems, with a focus on stereo downmix phase cancellation, cymbals, vocals and biome-melody integrity. Quality presets are adjustable but have not been accepted by Marvin.
2. **Delivery integration:** source-bound versioned variant URLs must be added to optional per-song download manifests; stage Lite before Full, verify source and derivative hashes, then cache either quality under unchanged logical playback URLs. The test worker from PR #179 presently checks complete original-recording groups only and does not yet know how to prioritize Lite/Full. It must never serve half-transferred groups or switch quality mid-segment; offline failure remains honest 503 instead of fallback synth.
3. **Gameplay spatialization:** in #180, add per-stem Web Audio pan control for BSOD bass, counter-moving drums, rider+airtime vocals and beat/trick-linked guitar/piano/Other. That work is cosmetic and independent of audio encoding, and beat grids remain unverified until #50.
4. **Compatibility and safety:** full-song/biome transitions, exact-frame alignment, versioned replays and browser devices; production service worker and cache UI only after review. No live app code or service-worker registration is changed in this PR.

Keep parent #172 **open**. This is a source-safe exploration stacked on #179, which itself is a draft stacked on #175 and #173.# #172 — Non-deployable offline prototype, original music only

**STATUS: experimental developer tooling.** The game **does not** register this worker or call the music downloader. This worktree is intentionally separate from #173 (install manifest/icons). Do not claim the game is an offline-ready PWA, and do not deploy the worker before a separate review and runtime integration PR.

## Owner's soundtrack requirement (2026-11-21)

- Never replace a selected real song with fallback/synthesized music just because the player is offline.
- The offline **required set** is every song that is **both unlocked and enabled**. Other songs need not download until requested online.
- If a player unlocks or enables a missing song while offline, retain their setting and show **DOWNLOAD REQUIRED WHEN ONLINE**; continue with other available real tracks or silence. A previously downloaded/cached song can play even when re-enabled offline.
- A full offline-ready indicator is **false** when an enabled/unlocked song is missing or incomplete; a user who deliberately turns all tracks off can play without downloading music.
- **Owner clarification: upcoming unlock bonuses should be prefetched BEFORE the unlock, not only once the player unlocks them.** When online and the player has consented to music downloads, opportunistically stage the next-to-unlock biome's real music assets for enabled songs. Current playable music gets priority. These advance/bonus downloads **must not be required** for the current "offline-ready" status and must not fetch music for OFF songs. The initial Debutmont menu lead is already required, so the next prefetch target is the first other upcoming biome.
- If an upcoming bonus does not finish preloading and unlock happens offline, give the honest download-required state; never silently substitute fake music.
- For today, all four soundtrack songs are available and **enabled by default**; current progression unlocks *original vocals* separately, not songs. A future song unlock needs a distinct entitlement input. Do not wrongly gate a song on `musicVocals`.
- Only after explicit metered-data/storage consent should we fetch large offline song packs. Do not auto-download a 429 MiB soundtrack merely because a PWA was installed. The device must explain storage and quota risks.

## Real published asset inventory (current repo)

Source: `public/music/catalog.json`, actual normalized `pack.json` and their exact referenced Ogg files, biome/adaptive sources and caption asset. Checked on CT223 using full-media checkout, not merely the sparse review worktree:

| Song | Files (incl. pack manifest) | Exact bytes | MiB |
|---|---:|---:|---:|
| Pope Leo Wants the AI Root Password | 1,315 | 118,992,984 | 113.5 |
| One More Run | 1,096 | 110,195,263 | 105.1 |
| Every Hacker (Runs Out of Code) | 1,242 | 118,136,679 | 112.7 |
| Varmont Is Saved | 1,096 | 102,241,295 | 97.5 |
| **All on, all available** | **4,749** | **449,566,221** | **428.7** |

This excludes unrelated unreferenced music review files, master FLAC and alternative performance experiment JSON. Song caches are *per song*, not one monolithic app cache. Because normal playback accesses segment and adaptive variant filenames, the first conservative candidate inventory includes **all referenced real recordings within a selected song**. Later per-feature optimisation must preserve the original audio mix and pass equivalent offline playback tests.

## Prototype modules and safety bounds

- `tools/pwa/build-offline-worker.mjs`: hash-pins the small built shell (HTML, JS, menu icons, music catalog); rejects mismatched byte content and does not mix game versions. It has **no game registration path**. The browser proof used an **explicit, manual test-only registration** and confirmed offline title launch; that is not a complete music-offline claim.
- `tools/pwa/music-download-plan.mjs`: chooses only unlocked+ON catalog tracks, resolves their complete original audio and adaptive assets, and builds real SHA-256 integrity/version inventories. The song unlock list is an explicit *future* parameter; null means all current songs available.
- `tools/pwa/music-groups.mjs`: selects mandatory real base stems, menu/available-biome melodies and Dynamic Orchestra alternates only when enabled; it also returns a **low-priority bonus lookahead for the next locked biome** before the unlock. Prefetch remains optional, current offline readiness stays independent.
- `tools/pwa/music-cache-prototype.mjs`: only when deliberately called by a future download UI, checks hashes and stores song bytes in a per-song CacheStorage namespace; partial transfers are resumable but never carry a complete marker; missing or corrupt files make `isRealSongDownloaded` false. Cached but temporarily disabled tracks are retained rather than silently destroyed.
- **No live audio routing yet.** The current `StemMusicDirector` still fetches song assets over standard URLs. Future versioned SW routing must only serve a song from its verified complete cache, and must avoid procedural fallback for missing selected tracks. This is an outstanding acceptance criterion, not delivered behavior.
- **No automatic worker activation or old-cache eviction.** Do not call `skipWaiting`, `clients.claim` or delete older version caches while an older client could be running. Define safe-boundary cache cleanup after the replay contract is stable. Cache quota/eviction must never remove saves or corrupt scores.

## Verified now

- Jest: installability, offline worker and song-selection/integrity/cache-downloader tests.
- Full-media source inventory: 4 real songs, **all 4,749 referenced files found**. A first-song version inventory hashed all 1,315 files and produced version `0e06c275cab122f7df3d`.
- Real Chromium: developer-only worker manually registered against locally built Vite preview; title scene reached after disconnecting the network, with no JS errors. This verifies the small shell only; no false audio claim.

## Remaining before actual PWA deployment

1. Independent review of #173 and of this experimental logic, then land safe additive slices.
2. Install/download UX with explicit 100+ MiB per-song size, consent, progress, cancellation and quota handling.
3. Offline-ready indicator from current enabled/unlocked set, per-song full hash verification and download-required messaging. Wire **existing real soundtrack engine** to verified cached Ogg/pack assets through a safely scoped service worker; preserve original stem mixing/adaptive audio. Do not use synth fallback for a selected missing track.
4. Release upgrades retaining old code/asset versions until live runs finish, and retaining the downloaded song cache across game updates.
5. On actual Android/ChromeOS/desktop/iOS devices: complete one or more required song downloads **online**, disconnect, boot, play a full run with **actual audible song stem audio**, switch ON an uncached track offline and verify explicit warning/no fake music, restore network and download, then update and retest saves/ghosts and recordings.

The parent issue **#172 remains OPEN** after any foundation slice merges.# #172 — Real Opus offline routing proof (developer-only)

**Status: experimental draft; no production registration, player download UI, or shipped offline promise.** This branch stacks on the independent offline-cache prototype (#175), which itself stacks on manifest/install shell (#173). No `src/**` production gameplay, soundtrack engine, replay, score or save code is changed. This work adds only developer tooling, a generated test-only worker, tests, and documentation.

## What was proved

- Generated a selected soundtrack inventory from `public/music/catalog.json` and the original `pack.json` files. The inventory honors enabled songs, actual currently usable biomes, Dynamic Orchestra OFF/ON, and opportunistic pre-unlock next-biome bonuses. It fingerprints the **original Opus/Ogg compressed recording bytes** with SHA-256; no transcription, synth substitute, re-encode or remote API is involved.
- The existing selected-real-song downloader caches original pack metadata and stem recordings in per-group CacheStorage namespaces. **Manifest containing exact URL/length/hash is written before the final completeness marker.** Offline readiness additionally requires the integrity manifest, marker and all verified byte contents to exist.
- The manually registered test-only service worker intercepts the **unchanged same-origin music URLs** used by the existing `StemMusicDirector`. It serves only whole, verified group caches. A missing/incomplete/corrupt cache group returns a clear 503 when offline instead of emitting substitute audio; it does not synthesize or cache live network responses. Other music is fetched only when online. Existing service-worker code retains safe versioning/no `skipWaiting`/no early cache deletion.
- **A real Chromium end-to-end test** launched a production build, manually registered the worker, selected *One More Run* only, downloaded the full original base-stem and Debutmont-lead groups (**10.06 MiB / 121 real referenced files**), severed browser networking, and reloaded the actual game. The actual `AudioSynth`/`StemMusicDirector` was initialized and reached **playing** with seven decoded original buffers (six stems + Debutmont lead), running Web Audio context and **no active procedural fallback music timer**. Two independently fetched real Ogg files matched their SHA-256 and decoded as 12-second stereo audio. An uncached OFF song returned 503. Deleting one of the lead group's cached files invalidated that entire group; another previously cached lead file also returned 503 until repaired. **Zero page errors.**

This is stronger than a standalone codec test: the actual game's music engine could use the original offline sources without modifying its transport. It is not yet a complete user-friendly production-ready offline music system. Automated audible waveform or multi-biome full-run checks, browser/device coverage, persistent settings UX, quota handling, version-upgrade and save/ghost retention all remain open.

## Reproduce on CT223 or a developer machine

A full-media checkout is required because CT223's developer worktrees intentionally sparse-exclude `.ogg` files. The test-only Node HTTP server maps `/music/packs/*.ogg` to the full-media source tree and serves the built app and its generated service worker from the isolated worktree. Nothing is installed on the Netlify site or production origin.

```sh
npm run build
BSB_MEDIA_PUBLIC_ROOT=/path/to/full-checkout/public CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/chromium \
  node tools/pwa/test-real-offline-audio.mjs
npm test -- --runInBand tests/src/pwa-music-integrity-groups.test.js \
  tests/src/pwa-music-cache.test.js tests/src/pwa-offline-worker.test.js \
  tests/src/pwa-music-groups.test.js tests/src/pwa-music-download-plan.test.js \
  tests/src/pwa-install-shell.test.js
```

Do **not** manually register this worker against a production origin. The test uses a fresh ephemeral localhost port and isolated browser context, whose test caches go away when Chromium closes.

## Hard remaining integration gates

1. **Consent-first per-song/per-biome download UI** for the already-selected original music, with progress, estimated bytes, cancellation, interrupted-resume, storage quotas, and honest no-music state. Never silently prefetch 429 MiB; unlocked+ON songs and currently usable real biome leads are mandatory, next pre-unlock biome is a low-priority, opt-in bonus.
2. **Production-only worker lifecycle** with deterministic-playback/conformance, preview and developer-test isolation. The full song must stay on the existing real stem engine; missing selected songs may not trigger the fallback synthesizer. Do not interrupt active run or break save/replay contracts on a worker update.
3. **Real devices and failure tests:** offline gameplay across segments and biomes, source and mix continuity, enabled song switching while offline, optional Dynamic Orchestra arrangements, cache eviction/repair, iOS storage policies, battery/data usage, ChromeOS/Android, uninstalled/reinstalled web app, and persisted progress/ghosts.

