- `stallProbe` reports recovery behaviour, not a score, so its score columns are expected to be low.# Music Friday → Control Friday: verified on-beat Gym release

**Owner intent (Marvin, 2026-11-18):** After approximately 1 second of staged READY, release the rider on a *regular musical pulse* where possible. Not specifically beat 1 of a bar; never target a syncopated offbeat. Applies to initial launches and quick retries. No musical requirement may hold the physics clock hostage.

## Music Friday deliverables

1. Create a *validated quarter-note pulse grid* for each active recording/arrangement (source-performance seconds). Either a confidently measured BPM **plus phase** for steady tempo, or actual ordered pulse timestamps for tempo-variable tracks. BPM alone is insufficient.
2. Record the grid's origin/version, confidence, manual spot-check outcome and track identity. Mark uncertain, silent, pickup-driven, rubato, and syncopated sections invalid until reviewed. Do **not** fabricate quarter-note pulses from bars, drum transients, or arbitrary tempo estimates.
3. Expose a **public, read-only timing API** on the music service (not the private `_stemMusic` field) that tells gameplay whether a grid is trusted, which track/transport revision it belongs to, and when the **next regular pulse** occurs relative to the actual playing transport / `AudioContext.currentTime`. Account for pause/resume, buffering/suspension, seeking, track changes, re-entries and loops.
4. Supply deterministic unit tests and one running-browser test with real playback, seeking, pause/resume, silent/muted music, low-confidence tracks, and mid-READY track changes. Include sample-accurate phase checks at beginning, middle, and near end of songs.

## Control Friday integration contract

- Gym's gameplay timer remains authoritative. Hold the first 1000 ms (normal READY) independently of music or WebAudio.
- **After** minimum hold, use the next *verified* regular pulse only if its extra wait is <=300 ms. Otherwise GO at once. A new/invalid track, disabled music, suspended audio, stale transport snapshot, missing API, exception or rejected timing always means immediate normal GO.
- Never wait for measure start, lock to upbeat subdivisions, step Matter physics from audio, reset the musical transport, or play/start a new track just to synchronize Gym.
- Preserve one uninterrupted *in-Gym* flight's five scored Bluffs for the hidden configuration unlock, and all existing non-ranked retry/mission/save isolation.

## Integration follow-through (2026-11-18)

The public music API from Music Friday commit `5325f5b` has been integrated on
`integration/gym-verified-beats-20261118`. RunScene owns the original READY,
queries at the boundary only, may add one bounded 0–300ms hold for a verified
pulse, and releases unconditionally even if the playback revision changes.
Collision resets and scene shutdown discard pending advice. All production
beat grids remain unverified, so the current release still uses normal
one-second GO. Browser-only synthetic grids exercise the trusted positive
path and actual original OGG transport without asserting musical correctness.
See the appended integration section in `src/ARCHITECTURE.md` and
`docs/MUSIC-GYM-VERIFIED-BEAT-TIMING-20261118.md` for the music-owned contract.# Issue #115: fair input timestamps

Status: implemented on `friday/issue-115-teamb-fair-input-20261121` (Team B), draft PR #162. Base `main` b51ab36.

## Problem (proved, not assumed)

The fixed 120 Hz tick (#59) made one input tape replay identically at any display rate, but live input
still reached the ticks through ONE poll per display frame:

| Finding | Status on main | Evidence |
|---|---|---|
| (a) one poll fanned to N ticks: a press is applied to the frame's first tick wherever it fell in the frame | **Proved.** At 30 Hz a press is applied up to 3 ticks (25 ms of simulated time) *before* the key went down; 60 Hz up to 1. A double tap inside one 30 Hz frame merges into one hop. | `tests/determinism/fair-input.test.js` (legacy block) |
| (b) held-only buttons (push, tuck, tilt, brake) tapped and released inside one frame are lost | **Proved.** A 20 ms push tap is lost in 87 of 240 phase-swept presses at 30 Hz (36 %). | latency sweep, below |
| (c) 0-tick frames (> 120 Hz, e.g. 144 Hz: 24 of 144 frames run no tick) carry edges forward but drop held state | **Proved.** A brake tap spanning a 0-tick frame is lost; the hop edge survives. | `(c)` tests |
| (d) a hop edge can arrive with `pad.hop` false | **Proved** for every sub-frame Space tap. Harmless to the jump itself (`TUNING.hopMinimumHoldMs` already gives a minimum hold) but the pad is self-contradictory. | `(d)` tests |
| (e) blur / visibilitychange release keys but not latched edges; tab suspension | **Confirmed, kept.** An edge latched before a blur is delivered once on the next frame. After a stall the resume frame runs the 6-tick cap (backlog dropped) and input from before the stall lands on its first tick: stale but never lost, never fast-forwarded. Unchanged by this PR (open question below). | `fair-input-edges.test.js` |
| (f) gamepad sampled per frame only | **Platform limitation, documented.** The Gamepad API has no per-button event timestamps; changes are seen when polled (prestep / poll). They keep exactly the pre-#115 schedule (first tick of the frame). | `(f)` test |

## Fix

* `InputRouter` (keyboard) and `TouchOverlay` (touch buttons: pointerdown / move / up / cancel and
  lostpointercapture) pass each event's `event.timeStamp` (the `performance.now()` clock, the same clock
  as the rAF time Phaser hands to `RunScene.update(time)`) to `VirtualPad.setButton(..., timeStamp)`.
  A timestamp not on that clock (legacy epoch values, synthetic events, jsdom) is treated as untimed
  (`PadDerive.eventTimeOnClock`). The overlay counts fingers per button, so multi-touch on one button is
  one hold; a touch and a key on the same button are one aggregate hold (released by the last one).
* `VirtualPad` journals every aggregate hold change since the last poll; `poll()` returns it as a
  non-enumerable `timeline` (snapshot shape unchanged; capped at 256 entries between polls).
* `SimClock.padsFor(pad, ticks, { frameTime, timeline, normalize })` gives each change to the tick whose
  real-time window contains it. This frame's ticks end at `frameTime - accMs - (ticks-1-i) * tickMs`.
  * Clamped into **this frame's ticks**: older than the first window (stall, untimed gamepad/blur)
    -> first tick (= old behaviour); newer than the last window (sub-tick remainder) -> last tick. So no
    input is ever shown a frame later than before, and no tick that already ran is rewritten.
  * Arrival order is kept **per button**: a change never lands before an earlier change of the same
    button, but an untimed gamepad change is never pushed behind a key/touch event on another button
    (it keeps the frame's first tick, as on main). Review finding #1, test-covered.
  * A button that went down inside a tick's window is held on that tick even if released before the
    window closed: a tap is at least one tick. Edges are rebuilt from the rises (so the hop edge tick
    always has `hop` held).
  * A 0-tick frame carries its timeline forward intact; a poll consumed in between (pause frame) is
    bridged with untimed changes so no hold goes stale.
  * Without a timeline (`TapePlayer` replays, probes that stub `poll`), `padsFor` is the old fan-out.
* `RunScene.update` passes `time`, the poll's timeline and `normalizePad` (re-run per tick).
* `deriveHeld` lives in the pure `systems/PadDerive.js`, so SimClock no longer imports VirtualPad /
  TouchOverlay. Switching between the timed and legacy paths right after a 0-tick frame keeps the
  carried edges.

## Measurements

Phase sweep: 240 W presses held 20 ms, 100 ms apart, each at a different phase of the frame
(`measureLatency`, `INPUT_TIMING_REPORT=1`). Tick lag = first tick holding push minus the tick whose
window contains the keydown (negative = applied to simulated time before the key was pressed).
Frame lag = render frame first showing it minus the first frame that could poll it.

| Rate | Path | Lost taps | Press tick lag min/mean/max | Release tick lag | Frame lag max |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| 30 Hz | main | 87/240 | -3 / -2.27 / -1 | -2 / -0.73 / 0 | 0 |
| 30 Hz | #115 | 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |
| 60 Hz | main | 0 | -1 / -0.49 / 0 | -1 / -0.51 / 0 | 0 |
| 60 Hz | #115 | 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |
| 120 Hz | main | 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |
| 120 Hz | #115 | 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 / 0 / 0 | 0 |
| 144 Hz | main | 0 | -1 / -0.35 / 0 | -1 / -0.31 / 0 | 1 (0-tick frames) |
| 144 Hz | #115 | 0 | -1 / -0.35 / 0 | -1 / -0.31 / 0 | 1 (0-tick frames) |

**Display latency added: none.** For every press the main path delivered, the #115 path shows it in the
same render frame (asserted per press at all four rates). On this steady, idealized cadence the
simulated-time lag goes from "up to 3 ticks early" to exact.

### Real game (Chromium, built bundle, `tools/fair-input-browser-probe.mjs`)

**Idealized clock** (the probe pins `event.timeStamp` and calls `game.step(T, dt)` directly, so there is
no rAF jitter and no Phaser delta smoothing). Real `KeyboardEvent`s, loop asleep, exact frame schedules,
seed FAIR115, 720 ticks, 18 key events including a 2.4 ms hop tap, a tuck tap, a brake tap and a
sub-frame double tap (the touch rows of the current probe are listed after the table):

| Build | 30 Hz | 60 Hz | 120 Hz | 144 Hz |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| main b51ab36: tape rows / end x | 16 / 2593.688 | 16 / 2593.688 | 16 / 2593.496 | 16 / 2596.174 |
| #115: tape rows / end x | 20 / 2602.739 | 20 / 2602.739 | 20 / 2602.739 | 20 / 2606.021 |

#115: identical ghost-tape input rows and identical sled state at 30/60/120 Hz. main: three different
tapes, three taps dropped at every rate (16 rows vs 20).

Touch is now in the same probe: real `PointerEvent`s (pointerType `touch`) at the overlay buttons' centres,
a 2.4 ms BRAKE tap, two fingers on HOP and a TUCK hold ended by `pointercancel`. With keyboard + touch
(27 tape rows) this PR gives identical tapes and sled state at 30/60/120 Hz (`FAIR_INPUT_PASS`, end x
2593.025); 144 Hz differs by the documented <= 1-tick-early rows.

### Real rAF loop (`REAL_RAF=1`)

Phaser's own requestAnimationFrame loop with real vsync times and TimeStep delta smoothing, Playwright
`page.keyboard` (browser-stamped events), 60 W presses per run. Each press is classified against the tick
windows of the frame that applied it (0 = its own tick, +n = n ticks early, -n = applied on the frame's
first tick although the event predates that frame's simulated window: the frame came late and the
backlog was dropped / smoothed away, which is not added delay since it is the first frame that could
show it). The container is a poor stand-in for a real display: swiftshader WebGL, median frame 50-67 ms,
stalls up to 1.2 s, so most frames run the 6-tick cap.

| Build | Runs x presses | Exact tick | Early (max) | Older than window |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| main b51ab36 | 1 x 60 | 4 | 36 (6) | 20 |
| #115 | 3 x 60 | 90 | 33 (6, once; otherwise <= 3) | 57 |

So on a real loop the scheduler is not exact: an event stamped after the frame's vsync time but delivered
before its callback goes to that frame's last tick. It is never late and never retroactive, and it is
much closer to exact than main. On a healthy 60 Hz display the callback runs within a few ms of vsync, so
the expected bound there is ~1 tick early; this container could not measure that directly.

## Limits and trade-offs

* **144 Hz (any rate that does not divide 120)** cannot be exact without adding latency: an event in
  the sub-tick remainder of a frame (after the last tick boundary it simulates, before the frame) is
  applied to the last tick, at most 1 tick early (35 % of presses in the sweep, exactly as before).
  Deferring it to its own tick would show it one display frame (6.9 ms) later. Not done; open question.
* Phaser's `smoothStep` may make a frame's simulated time differ slightly from its wall interval; the
  mapping is per frame and clamped, so this only moves events between that frame's own ticks.
* **Real rAF / delta smoothing (honest bound).** Under a real loop the rAF `time` is the frame's vsync
  time, and keydown is not rAF-aligned: a key stamped after `T` but delivered before that frame's
  callback goes to the last tick, i.e. up to ~1 tick early at **any** rate (also 60 Hz) on a normal
  machine, more if the callback runs long after vsync. It is never late and never retroactive.
* Gamepad changes are untimed (first tick of the frame, as before): the Gamepad API has no per-button
  event times. Device-orientation tilt (analog) is also untimed.
* Edges latched before a blur or a tab suspension are still delivered on resume (unchanged).
* **Cross-source mixing inside one frame.** The two sources of one button are a single aggregate hold, and
  only the timed source is placed inside the frame. If a key on a button is released mid-frame (timed, say
  tick 3) and the gamepad reports the same button pressed at poll time (untimed, tick 1), the per-button
  order puts the gamepad press at tick 1 and the key release after it, so the aggregate stays held; but a
  *different* gamepad control read at poll time (for example the opposite tilt direction) lands on tick 1
  while the key is still held until tick 3, so ticks 1-2 can briefly combine both (tilt back + forward =
  tuck, tilt 0). This needs an opposite-direction switch from keyboard to gamepad inside one frame (at most
  3 ticks at 30 Hz, 1 at 60 Hz); it ends by the frame's last tick and never affects stored tapes.

## Replay / tape compatibility

* Replay Contract v1.0 (#152, Team A) classifies this change as a recorder-side capture policy: tapes stay
  `bb-pad` v1, and the manifest field `info.inputCapture.edgeAssignment` is `frame-first-tick` on main and
  `timestamp-window` under #115. `SimClock.js` exports `INPUT_EDGE_ASSIGNMENT = 'timestamp-window'` for the
  #164 manifest writer to read; nothing here writes manifests. No new recorded quantity, so no protocol bump.

* Ghost tape format, header, `GHOST_TAPE_VERSION` and `GHOST_PHYSICS_VERSION` unchanged; `GhostTape.js`
  untouched. Existing tapes replay tick-for-tick identically (`TapePlayer` does not use `padsFor`).
* **Live recordings change**: for the same physical input, the per-tick pads recorded from now on are the
  fair ones (taps kept, presses on their own tick). This changes what a player's live run does, not how
  any stored tape replays. Flagged for #152 (Team A).

Diagnostic tooling only (`tests/determinism/*`, `tools/fair-input-browser-probe.mjs`): digests there are
non-authoritative and to be replaced by the #152 contract when published.# #126: Seeded hop-noise panels

This developer-only simulation compares stress profiles on the same courses.
It is not live gameplay input, a population estimate, or a competitive run.

```sh
node tools/sim/run.mjs \
  --noiseProfiles ideal,steady,distracted \
  --seeds sim-01,sim-02,sim-03 --maxM 500 --maxSec 25 \
  --verify --out bot-panel.json
```

Use the existing Vite/Playwright harness setup. For an installed Chromium,
set `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE` if needed. Without `--noiseProfiles`, the other
simulation policies and their defaults are unchanged. A noise panel uses
2–3 distinct profiles and 1–12 distinct seeds. By default it uses three
seeds, 500 m and 25 simulated seconds. The bounds are 5,000 m and 60 seconds.
It uses serial, fresh disposable browser contexts, no UI or tab reuse, and
pose observations every 60 harness frames. Page resources are restricted to the
local Vite origin; redirects, WebSockets and service workers are blocked.

## What the profiles change

The existing reactive `hop` policy emits a one-shot hop intent. The profiles
can delay it by an integer number of **60 Hz harness frames** or omit it:

| Profile | Delay after an intent | Independently selected miss rate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `ideal` | 0 frames | 0% |
| `steady` | Uniform 3–7 frames (50–117 ms) | 8% |
| `distracted` | Uniform 8–18 frames (133–300 ms) | 35% |

These are illustrative parameters, not measured reaction times. The course
seed, profile name, model version and `--noiseSeed` label seed an independent
reaction stream; the physics RNG does not supply these draws. A delivered hop
occupies one harness frame and presses only on that frame's first fixed tick.
The simulator continues to run its ordinary **120 Hz fixed ticks**. If the
sled's state changes before a delayed press, the real game decides whether it
has any effect. The planner does not add extra control presses, cross-button
mistakes, or fatigue. Overlapping intents while one is pending are counted and
coalesced instead of emitting simultaneous hops.
The diagnostics count intents, misses, coalesced intents, and sent presses;
`delayFrames` is total scheduled delay, including a press still pending at
the end if there is one.

All profiles use the same selected biome mask, motion rule, hop policy
settings, and empty boosts/upgrades. Existing harness timekeeping and stop
priority are reused. A pass reaches the configured forward distance without a
crash on the frame that reaches it. Crashes, the simulated timeout, and stalls
are separately classified failures and stay in the denominator. The summary
is an observed distribution for this small panel, not a claim about people
or relative profile difficulty on all terrain.

## Repeatability evidence

Each simulated run uses the game's existing replay isolation path so it does
not save a personal score or tape. The report captures the input passed
to the actual fixed ticks using the existing ghost input encoder. Rows are
`[tickDelta, padBits, tilt127]`, including the neutral boot ticks, paired with
their codec version, tick rate, terminal tick count, sample poses, and exact
terminal observations. They are diagnostic input rows, not complete
shareable ghost tapes.

With `--verify`, each course/profile trial runs a second time with the
same seeded planner and separately replays the **captured input rows** in a
fresh browser context through the existing tape player with no noise
planner. Both must reproduce actual fixed-tick input rows, pose samples,
terminal result and harness observations. Timing data and the unavailable
planner counters on tape playback are not compared. Verification fails
the command if either check differs. The optional output file records the
inputs and comparison result; it is not submitted to any leaderboard or
proof of cross-version or cross-hardware equivalence.# #126: compare reaction seeds across matched bot panels

Use this developer-only report to find courses where the same synthetic hop policy reaches the distance threshold with one reaction RNG label but not another. This complements the single-panel #70 shortlist. It does not select or publish an official Daily, replay the trials, modify any game path, rank courses, or estimate human fairness.

Produce the panels from the **same checkout/build and browser environment**, changing only `--noiseSeed`. Example from the repository root:

```sh
build=$(git rev-parse HEAD)
node tools/sim/run.mjs --verify --seeds sim-01,sim-02,sim-03 \
  --policy hop --noiseProfiles ideal,steady,distracted --maxM 500 --maxSec 25 \
  --noiseSeed bb-noise-1 --out /tmp/bb-noise-a.json
node tools/sim/run.mjs --verify --seeds sim-01,sim-02,sim-03 \
  --policy hop --noiseProfiles ideal,steady,distracted --maxM 500 --maxSec 25 \
  --noiseSeed independent-review --out /tmp/bb-noise-b.json
node tools/sim/compare-noise-seeds.mjs --build "$build" \
  --panel /tmp/bb-noise-a.json --panel /tmp/bb-noise-b.json
```

- Pass 2–8 regular local UTF-8 JSON panel files (at most 4 MiB each; 32 MiB total input). Use distinct reaction labels and simple course/reaction IDs, 1–64 ASCII letters, digits, dots, underscores or hyphens, starting with a letter or digit. Use the full 40-hex commit of the producing checkout, not the report tool's checkout if they differ.
- Only complete classic v1 hop-noise panels with successful runner-reported seeded repeat and input playback are accepted; unverified, fatigue, partial, and derived reports are not accepted. All panels must have the same course set, profile definitions, pass distance, time limit, biome mask, movement rule and hop lead. Unrecognized panel/settings fields are rejected rather than silently ignored.
- For each course, the full recorded `ideal` trial must match, not just its pass result. A changed input row, trace, final state or swapped control invalidates the comparison. Panel order, seed order, and JSON object key order do not change how trials are matched.
- Each profile has pass/non-pass counts per reaction label over the whole matched course set. A flip lists a course with both a pass and a non-pass across tested labels and identifies the labels that passed. Two different kinds of non-pass are not a flip. Different labels can produce the same effective actions. No rates are extrapolated beyond those tested labels and courses.
- The JSON report hashes the exact bytes read for every panel. The source Git commit is caller-asserted, and the comparison does not authenticate source files, independently rerun verification, or prove the browser/build settings. The CLI writes the report to stdout only; on failure it writes a diagnostic to stderr and exits nonzero. Input paths, scores, clocks, poses, input rows, traces and per-course raw trial records are not copied to the result.# #126: Optional simulated-time fatigue for hop-noise panels

Add `--fatigue` to the existing [local bot panel](issue-126-human-noise-bots.md)
to model weaker reactions later in the simulated course. It is an opt-in
**synthetic QA workload**, not human data, a production feature, or a score
or difficulty claim.

```sh
node tools/sim/run.mjs --noiseProfiles ideal,steady,distracted \
  --seeds sim-01,sim-02,sim-03 --maxM 500 --maxSec 25 \
  --fatigue --verify --out fatigue-panel.json
```

Use `--fatigue` with no value and only with `--noiseProfiles`. Omit it for the
original v1 workload. `ideal` is unchanged even with the flag. For `steady`
and `distracted`, a new hop intent whose response can be scheduled uses the
current **60 Hz simulated harness frame**, not wall time. At frame 360 (6 s)
there is still no addition. From there to frame 1080 (18 s) the ramp rises
linearly, adding up to 6 whole delay frames (100 ms, rounded down) and up to
16 percentage points of miss probability. It stays capped afterward. The
underlying physics still advances at 120 fixed ticks per simulated second.

The original course/profile reaction seed and draws are reused. Paired runs
use the same draws until the first changed decision, after which different
branches or course states can change what is drawn. This does not consume
global or physics randomness. The ramp is sampled when a new intent is
considered; it cannot change a pending press, stack presses, or add other
controls. A fresh trial starts at frame zero.

## Reading the output

The optional report uses kind `local-tool-assisted-fatigue-bot-panel` and
per-trial noise model version 2, with the exact ramp in `settings.fatigue`.
It is deliberately distinct from v1, so the #70 legacy-only shortlist tool
will reject it rather than mislabel it. Without the flag the original v1
report kind, shape, decision behavior and defaults are unchanged. Neither
mode changes the production saved-tape format.

Under each opt-in trial's planner statistics, `fatigue.phaseIntents` counts
new, non-coalesced intents before the ramp, during the ramp, or once capped.
It counts attempts to schedule, including misses. `fatigue.addedDelayFrames`
is only the extra delay scheduled after successful draws, including a press
still pending at the end; it is not an observed player response time. The
unaffected `ideal` row explicitly has `applies: false` and no fatigue counts.

The existing `--verify` process still uses fresh disposable browser contexts
for an independent seeded repeat and for playback of the actual captured
fixed-tick input rows. Both must reproduce the observations and terminal result.
Only playback-only planner counters and wall time are excluded from the
applicable comparison. No changes to scoring, storage, live physics, or
leaderboards are made.# #127: one stock Packet Spike rollout

This developer-only check compares three fixed inputs at **one naturally generated hazard**. It complements the geometry-only terrain preflight but does not exercise its `fair15` or `fair4` examples or establish a link to any geometry flag. It is not a timing-window map, fairness verdict, course-wide reachability check, or ranked result.

Build `0c8c6c1d9b272fb19d8dba36322e0e26448d219d` (or a checkout with the exact same compiled game module), then serve its `dist` locally. Run from the checkout containing the script, with Playwright and Chromium installed. `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE` can select a compatible installed browser.

```sh
npm run build
npm run preview -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8293
# In another terminal:
node tools/qa/stock-hazard-rollout.mjs --url http://127.0.0.1:8293/
```

The script checks the served module bytes, not the provenance of the whole build or asset set. All arms start from tick zero on `sim-11`, mask 0, frozen UTC `2026-01-05T12:00:00Z` (Featherweight), default Trick Deck Right, normal non-Official play and no loadout. The preview uses normal fixed ticks (120 Hz) and real keyboard events through the game's input router in Chromium. After normal boot, the runner skips the title transition animation and uses Phaser's `headlessStep` to update scenes and simulation without drawing every tick. This is not a rendering measurement. It does not inject terrain, pose, velocity, hazard state, gear or collision outcomes.

The target is Packet Spike in chunk 5, generated spawn x=10,363 px. The script matches the same exact spawn and live sensor in all trials. The matching pre-input states, real hop pulse, and fatal-event entity identity are checked. All three arms collect the same natural Packet Burst speed pickup at tick 1425; its three-second effect expires before either scheduled input. The check requires that shared pickup and expiry, no other pickups, and no active speed burst at the approach. Shield protection, invulnerability, respawn, negated crash, missed ticks, and per-arm repetition differences cause failure.

| Arm | Space input | Observed outcome |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Neutral | None | The target kills on tick 2570. |
| Scheduled hop | Press at tick 2537, release 54 ticks later | One actual grounded takeoff, no target hit, trailing body bound passes the sensor's right bound by more than 20 px at tick 2590, good landing at tick 2645, still alive at the tick-3000 cap. |
| Late control | Press at tick 2568, same scheduled release | One actual grounded takeoff, but the same sensor kills on tick 2571 before release. |

Each arm must reproduce exactly in two fresh browser contexts. These are three observed input schedules, not the earliest or latest viable hop. The successful arm is only observed through the fixed horizon; nothing is asserted about its remainder.

Only same-origin loopback GET/HEAD is allowed; redirects, WebSockets and service workers are blocked. The stock game may write transient run or ghost data on fatal outcomes inside disposable contexts. Those contexts are closed; the tool uses no player profile, exports no raw tape, and transmits or retains no saves. It prints its local evidence to stdout and writes no report file. No game behavior or release version is changed.# #127: Offline blind-spacing terrain preflight

Use this developer CLI to reproduce seeded hazard spacing before deciding what
to test in the game. It calls the production `TerrainGenerator` with the same
option inputs as `RunScene`, loading the five sets of checked-out JSON
definitions that the game loads with Vite. It does not open a browser, run a
sled, write a save, or change terrain.

The mask is an unsigned decimal 32-bit bitfield. The zero-based origin chunk
range is `[fromChunk, fromChunk + chunkCount)`, defaulting to `[0, 80)`. A
request can cover at most 256 origin chunks and cannot end above chunk index
4096 (exclusive). The range selects **blind spawn origins only**. The tool
looks farther forward when needed to cover each origin's reference clearance,
including through a second later chunk and at exact equality. If geometry
would exceed 64 lookahead chunks, or a scan would exceed 4,096 reported
pairs, the command fails rather than silently dropping findings. Reports go
only to stdout; redirect it to save one.

## What a finding means

The report pairs a blind hazard with each crash-classified hazard whose
**spawn-center X** lies strictly ahead and no farther than the production
`blindClearAheadPx` reference distance, including placements in other chunks.
It also reports the separate nominal distance at the generator's expected
speed during the blind timer alone, without speed or reaction padding. The
production generator currently uses the padded rule within its ground-blind
placement pass; here it is a comparison reference for ground and air origins.

Each entry gives exact world X/Y, chunk index, and separation in pixels and
meters. Generator distance is world X divided by its scale; it is not HUD
distance from the spawned sled. `motionKind` shows a declared moving or gated
hazard, if any; we don't estimate where a rolling hazard ends up, when a gate
opens, where a sled lands, the actual hitbox clearance, or whether the blind
effect triggers. The `glitchChasm` name in a finding refers to the crash hazard
definition, not a measurement of arbitrary terrain gaps. This is **not** a
finding of unfairness or unreachability. Branch routing, stall traps, dynamic
motion, actual physics rollouts and reproducible tapes are not implemented.

## Reproduce the examples

Run the commands from the repository root at the indicated revision. Use a
separate checkout or worktree if you have local changes. Check the report's
`provenance.sha256` before comparing coordinates across revisions.

### SIM6 reference snapshot

At [`eb281d8`](https://github.com/truevox/BitstreamBluffs/commit/eb281d8ee34c51c16c10dbe7735c4bcbab5aaede),
the input SHA-256 is
