## Observed local Chromium result

Both original captures had the same native starting header and encoded neutral
inputs. Each build matched two fresh replays at all 21 of its native
checkpoints. The first exact sampled state difference was at tick 1806. Over
the 2556 common completed ticks, the maximum position difference was 40.31 px;
each arm had 343 sampled airborne ticks, and score at the last common tick
was 612 in A and 614 in B. Both runs hit a Packet Spike. The fatal tick moved
from 2570 to 2557 (13 ticks earlier, about 108 ms at 120 Hz). The capture does
not determine why a particular contact diverged or generalize to other
courses, controls, engines or releases.# #131: airtime from one pinned historical replay

This developer pilot reads one existing synthetic recording,
`tools/conformance/fixtures/cross-engine/collision-heavy.tape.json`, from the
[cross-engine conformance fixtures](determinism/cross-engine-matrix.md).
It derives airtime from the original game's own grounded state; it does not
read a player recording, add telemetry or change replay compatibility.

## Reproduce

1. Check out the exact [recording source](https://github.com/truevox/BitstreamBluffs/commit/1e0e52469a7574893349b3f9afa8f0c90ccebe31)
   named in the fixture's `record-meta.json`:
   `1e0e52469a7574893349b3f9afa8f0c90ccebe31`. That checkout reports v2.16.0
   and simulation revision 1. The v2.18.1 label in the conformance report
   refers to its later matrix, not the recording checkout.
2. Install that checkout's locked dependencies, build it with `npm run build`,
   and serve the compiled preview on `127.0.0.1:8346`.
3. In the newer checkout containing this tool, use Playwright with Chromium and
   explicitly opt into the fixed fixture:

```sh
node tools/qa/historical-airtime-pilot.mjs --url http://127.0.0.1:8346/ --use-synthetic-fixture
```

The tool checks the fixture's exact bytes before parsing and checks the
compiled boot module, ruleset loader, active ruleset archive and live ruleset
ID before playback. It checks those runtime modules, not the provenance of
every served file. Use a local preview you built and trust. Unsupported
fixtures and newer runtime builds fail rather than opening a legacy tape in
the new game. There is no option to pass an arbitrary local tape.

Two fresh Chromium replays must agree at all 13 native cadence checkpoints
and on sampled state and groundedness at each of the 1,668 completed fixed
ticks. Airtime counts observed ticks where the native grounded flag was off,
converted at 120 Hz. An episode can overlap either observation boundary;
such an episode is not counted as a distinct takeoff-to-landing cycle.
No fatal tick is measured, and pose traces are not used to infer airtime.

On the pinned fixture the original-build check measured **418 airborne
ticks (3.483 seconds)** and **six observed episodes, four bounded by
grounded ticks**. This is fixed-tick simulation time, not wall time,
real-world air time or certification of any player result.

The bounded receipt reports aggregate numbers and the fixed pins, not raw
inputs, poses, local paths, seed, score, or profile information. It is not
general compatibility for older formats, cross-browser proof, or a public
replay verifier. The existing same-build airtime command is unchanged.# #131: Local airtime analytics from a saved replay

This developer diagnostic measures airtime from recorded inputs without adding
anything to the game's live telemetry. Use only a normal-run tape you have
permission to analyze and a local build from the same revision that recorded
it. Start the built game's loopback preview as in the
[#132 benchmark instructions](issue-132-deterministic-benchmarks.md), then run:

```sh
node tools/sim/benchmark-replay.mjs --url http://127.0.0.1:8293/ \
  --airtime-build "$RECORDED_BUILD_COMMIT" --tape /path/to/local-tape.json \
  --repeats 2 > replay-airtime.json
```

The flag and supplied file are explicit opt-in. The full 40-hex Git commit is
an assertion supplied by the caller, **not** authenticated by the tool. Replay
uses 2–5 independent, disposable Chromium contexts (three by default) and the
full recorded completed interval. There is no scripted fallback or `--ticks`
prefix in this mode. Without `--airtime-build`, the benchmark retains its
ordinary performance report and does not sample airtime.

## What is measured

After each successfully completed fixed tick, the reader samples
`RunScene.sled.grounded`, including the game's own ground-contact grace. An
`airborneTick` is a completed tick whose sampled state is not grounded.
`cumulativeAirborneMs` is that count converted at the fixed clock rate; it is
not elapsed wall time, a geometric estimate from sampled poses, or physical
airtime integrating pauses, hit-stop or slow motion. An episode is a contiguous
sequence of observed airborne ticks. `boundedByGround` counts only episodes
with a grounded observation on both sides. `startsAirborne` and `endsAirborne`
mark open observation boundaries, not a proved takeoff or landing. If the
entire interval is airborne, one episode touches both boundaries.

## Accepted tapes and evidence

Intake accepts at most 2 MiB of UTF-8 JSON and 21,600 completed ticks. It
requires current supported normal-run v1 fields: versions, explicit seed/mask,
recorded calendar and rule, control layout, recognized loadout and world
options, start pose and all RNG stream snapshots. The disposable registry uses
the recorded control layout without writing profile settings. The game must
reconstruct the recorded starting pose and options without a fallback. Daily,
Gym, past releases and incomplete files are not supported by this slice.

Every on-cadence checkpoint must be present, the first at tick 120, and every
row must have the current eight fields. Both the existing `TapePlayer` checks
and the benchmark's per-tick observations (including grounded state) must agree
for every repeat. Any early crash, uncovered checkpoint, saved score or tape,
or divergence fails with nonzero status and no successful report. The tape's
`endTick` is the last completed tick. An input change at `endTick+1` may be
present but is not executed. The report does not validate the fatal tick,
end-of-run conversion fields, all game state, or historical compatibility.

The preview must be trusted and on loopback. Cross-origin requests, redirects,
WebSockets and service workers are blocked. The input remains local, is used
only for playback in disposable contexts, and is not added to the game library.
The result contains aggregate data, observed compatibility versions and the
asserted commit. It contains no seed, timestamps, raw tape, commands, score,
local paths, or individual ground-state samples. It is not a leaderboard
result or attestation of a human run.# #132: Local replay performance benchmark

Run this against the minified game from the checkout being measured. Install
dependencies and Playwright Chromium before going offline. Use a separate terminal
for the preview server:

```sh
npm ci
npx playwright install chromium
npm run build
npm run preview -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8293 --strictPort
```

```sh
node tools/sim/benchmark-replay.mjs > replay-benchmark.json
```

The default workload plays the first nine seconds of the scripted GHOST56
opening (base course, no boosts/upgrades). It encodes those inputs using the
existing ghost-tape encoder, with no invented stored checkpoints. The replay
loads its seed and loadout through the normal `RunScene` tape path, stops the
browser's frame loop, and drives one fixed simulation tick per synthetic frame.
The calendar date is pinned because it affects the movement rule. The report
includes both the requested duration and the actual tick count: the default
stops early on a natural crash, and results with different actual tick counts
are not directly comparable.

To measure a saved **normal-run** ghost tape from the matching build instead:

```sh
node tools/sim/benchmark-replay.mjs --tape /path/to/ghost-tape.json --repeats 5 > saved-benchmark.json
```

Pass a raw ghost tape object, not a ledger record or export wrapper. The existing
compatibility check rejects mismatched format, physics, or tick versions, and
the harness also checks the run-random version. It sets the browser calendar to
the tape's `startedAt` if present (otherwise the fixed default), checks the
recorded movement rule when available, and requires the requested prefix to
complete without crashing. The recorded end tick is the last completed tick
*before* the crash. Use `--ticks N` to compare a common prefix within the tape;
short prefixes can contain no recorded checkpoints. Daily and Gym modes are not
supported. To supply a system Chromium instead of the Playwright download, set
`CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/chromium`.

## Reading the report

- For each trial, `simTick` reports count, total, and nearest-rank p95/p99
  **milliseconds per call** of `RunScene.simTick`. `simTicksPerSecond` is tick
  count divided by the sum of those calls' measured durations.
- `frame` measures synchronous `game.step` elapsed time.
  `frameOutsideSimTick` is per-frame subtraction, not an independent render
  measurement. It includes tape input/verification, scene updates, synchronous
  GPU submission, and measurement overhead. `frameTicksPerSecond` is not live
  browser FPS; it excludes waits between frames and does not wait for GPU
  completion, compositor presentation, or display refresh.
- `setupMs` separately measures context creation, page navigation, boot, and
  replay initialization. It excludes browser process launch. State snapshots,
  report calculation and JSON output are outside the measured step intervals.
- Keep the tape hash, requested/actual ticks, calendar/rule, game-entry filename
  (hashed by Vite), browser, renderer, viewport, and host metadata when comparing
  runs. The default forces SwiftShader for a software baseline. To try the
  machine's default GPU path, pass `--native-gpu` and verify `renderer` in the
  report. Compare repeated trials rather than treating one noisy sample as a
  regression; timer resolution, cache/JIT state and background load matter.
  Zero-duration measurements mean the clock could not resolve that interval;
  throughput is null if the entire sampled interval has zero duration.

## Isolation and limits

The harness creates a fresh browser context for the uninstrumented replay and
for every timed trial. Only the trusted loopback preview's origin can serve
page resources; redirected requests, WebSockets and service workers are
blocked. The tool does not use a live profile and never imports into the
production game. It only prints local development measurements.

The timing wrapper reads a monotonic clock before and after the original tick.
Neither clock reads nor statistics set the tick delta, input, loadout, or stop
condition. Every measured trial must match an independent replay without the
wrapper, tick by tick, on sled pose/velocity, exact score, scramble RNG state,
and crash status. Supplied checkpoints are also checked by the existing replay
path when reached. A mismatch exits nonzero rather than emitting a successful
report. The observation hash describes only the compared fields, not a complete
authoritative game-state digest; this is not cross-release/cross-browser
conformance, leaderboard verification, or a record of a human run.# #155: Ground hop-cue visibility baseline

This developer-only probe demonstrates a visual warning gap in the existing camera/telegraph path. It does **not** change that path, implement Pulp, establish how often live play hits the condition, or validate 100% Pulp. Keep #155 open.

