CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE=/path/to/chromium node tools/qa/hop-cue-visibility.mjs --url http://127.0.0.1:8455/
```

The runner checks the loaded entry bytes, not just the filename. The commit identifies where this baseline was checked; the one entry hash does not authenticate the entire checkout, dist directory, or public asset set. The browser gets a new disposable context per trial. Only GET/HEAD to the selected loopback origin are permitted; redirects, WebSockets and service workers are blocked. The probe does not save a run or ghost or export any images. Normal boot can initialize disposable settings.

## What it measures

Both fresh stock runs take the normal `sim-11` setup to tick 120, then freeze before fixtures are applied. A synthetic 40×24 ground hazard (not the stock Packet Spike geometry) is passed only through the actual `RunScene.updateGroundTelegraph` call; it is never added to the physics world. Two planner samples move its predicted arrival from the warn range into the hop window. The previous rendered right edge and the current render right edge are independently controlled; the rider remains in frame for the captured case. The injected framing (rider x≈415/437 in the two capture views) is **not** the default follow camera or a Pulp camera path. All arms use zero roll and zoom 1.

The actual `GroundTelegraph` graphic draws the column or the edge badge. The runner captures the real triangle supplied to Phaser, projects it through the camera matrix, and compares the composed WebGL framebuffer with just the telegraph graphic hidden. It checks that the two shown frames bracketing a hidden frame yield exactly the same pixels, that the unhidden frame is not blank, and that the positive controls change pixels inside the expected cue bounds. This is pixel evidence, not just a changed `onScreen` flag. Counts are indicative for the tested Chromium/GPU; assertions require the observed absence or presence, not specific counts across platforms.

| Render case | Cue triangles (screen x) | Observed pixels changed near cue |
| --- | --- | ---: |
| Current 480-pixel viewport, hazard spans x478–518 | Column, x489.75–506.25 | 0 |
| Same viewport, planner's narrower prior edge selects badge (positive control) | Edge, x445.75–462.25 | 140 |
| Camera retreats 64 px after prior frame classified the hazard on screen | Column, x511.75–528.25 | 0 |
| Same retreated viewport, matching planning edge selects badge (positive control) | Edge, x445.75–462.25 | 127 |

Every arm requests the same warn sound at 800 ms and hop sound at 1000 ms, with identical warning marker and predicted arrival (~1329.93 ms). This compares sound **requests**, not audio output. The sampled run tick, clock, sled pose and score stay unchanged; this is not a complete subsystem parity check. The report is emitted only after both fresh Chromium contexts reproduce the same observations.

For a future fix, preserve the existing camera-independent warning and hop schedule. Separately, decide badge versus column from the **current projected cue bounds** in the renderer, not the planner's stale view or whether the hazard's left edge merely overlaps the viewport. A real Pulp comparison still needs real canary runs, moving/rotating framing coverage, and the separately owned #140/#117/#118 work.# #157: stock corkscrew scoring baseline

This developer-only baseline captures the repeated corkscrew / airbrake / parachute scoring mechanism before any balance changes. It is a new scripted reproduction in the current stock game, not Colin's playtest footage, inputs, reported 500,000-point run, or near-ten-second flights. It makes no claim about humans' scoring rate, how often the pattern occurs, or what a rebalance should do.

Build the pinned source `8dd230bfc7db8f3eed7e73bb0a0b0fb92bfa982b` (or a checkout producing the identical bundled game) and serve it locally. Run the tool from the checkout containing this developer fixture, with the repository's Playwright dev dependency and Chromium. `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE` can point to a locally installed compatible browser.

```sh
npm run build
npm run preview -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8293
# In another terminal:
node tools/qa/trick-baseline.mjs --url http://127.0.0.1:8293/
```

The verifier checks the compiled entry path and served bundle SHA-256 in each fresh Chromium context; this identifies those bundle bytes, not an independently authenticated source checkout or asset set. It uses `sim-11`, mask 0, `2026-01-05T12:00:00Z` to select Featherweight, normal non-Official play, default Trick Deck Right and no gear. It skips the menu transition animation to start the normal scene at tick zero. The fixed fixture sends ordinary key down/up events through the live keyboard router at 120 Hz without changing terrain, velocity, input handling, rules or scoring. There is no adaptive search in the tool.

| Landing tick | Grade | Airtime (rounded) | Corkscrews | Banked trick points | Landing bonus |
| ---: | --- | ---: | ---: | ---: | ---: |
| 246 | Perfect | 2,050 ms | 4 | 3,680 | 20 |
| 608 | Good | 2,792 ms | 4 | 3,680 | 0 |
| 920 | Good | 2,375 ms | 4 | 3,680 | 0 |

Each flight also activates and scores both airbrake and parachute. Corkscrew event counts `1, 2, 3, 4` are cumulative within each flight, not ten tricks. The runner checks the landing event and actual scored balance together, including the one bonus: **11,060 banked trick/bonus points**, excluding distance. Two fresh Chromium runs with rendering enabled must match the expected events and each other exactly. Any crash, respawn, pickup, missed tick, score change or divergence fails the verifier.

Only same-origin loopback GET/HEAD is allowed; redirects, WebSockets and service workers are blocked. The run stops before Game Over, checks that no run or ghost tape was saved, and discards the browser context (including any store initialization). It prints JSON to stdout and does not publish, write a replay, alter persistent player data or submit a leaderboard score. This establishes one local pre-change baseline; it does not close the scoring-design issue.# #172: Installability foundation — phase 1

**Status:** This slice adds *install metadata and icons*. It is not an offline game yet, and it makes no promise about offline functionality. The service worker, atomic asset cache, safe updates, and persistent save/ghost upgrade testing remain in issue #172.

## Included

- `public/manifest.webmanifest`: same-origin app identity, standalone landscape presentation, name, color and first-party icon locations.
- `public/icons/bitstream-192.png`, `bitstream-512.png`, `bitstream-512-maskable.png`, and `apple-touch-icon.png`. They are generated from the game's own menu logo and sled-rider sprite layers, not third-party assets.
- `index.html`: manifest link, browser theme color and supported Apple home-screen tags.
- `tools/pwa/make-icons.mjs`: optional local regeneration command. Uses already-installed Playwright and first-party SVG sources; **not** part of build or game startup.
- `tests/src/pwa-install-shell.test.js`: manifest, icon dimensions, safe same-origin paths, Apple metadata and unchanged game boot wiring.

Regenerate after updating the menu art (requires locally installed Playwright Chromium):

```sh
CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE=/usr/bin/chromium node tools/pwa/make-icons.mjs
```

Validate the slice:

```sh
npm test -- --runInBand tests/src/pwa-install-shell.test.js
npm run build
npm run preview -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8293 --strictPort
```

On the local preview, inspect `/manifest.webmanifest`, all `/icons/` assets and the `index.html` manifest link. On supported mobile and desktop browsers, the manifest may enable an install option. This is not an assertion that all browsers will offer install UI: installation depends on browser and platform rules.

## Deliberately deferred

**No service worker is registered or added in this PR.** That means no offline shell, precaching, background fetches, update prompts or cache manipulation yet. The existing `main.js` entry and game runtime remain unmodified.

Follow-up implementation should be developed only after Teams A/B have stabilized the replay and browser-conformance contracts. The service worker must be opt-in for production and explicitly absent from local Vite dev, Netlify deploy previews and deterministic test harnesses. Release versions must not mix HTML or hashed assets across service-worker upgrades; activation must wait for safe game boundaries and preserve IndexedDB/localStorage saves, rankings and ghosts. Optional music and external services must degrade gracefully offline, rather than being naively preloaded. See the full acceptance matrix in issue #172.

**Parent issue #172 stays open after this additive slice merges.**# Issue #47: playful snow-racing Kodiaks

Status: **playable slice** on `feature/kodiaks-47`, for Marvin to playtest.

## Owner decisions

- **Rivals, not killers (owner override, 2026-11-21).** The bears are competitive, spirited racers. Contact
  shoves the sled back and up. It never crashes it.
- Three types, as Marvin named and coloured them:
  - **Kernel-Panic**: white, polar.
  - **Brute-Force**: brown grizzly.
  - **Black-Ice**: black, Vermont black bear.
- Kodiaks appear from **Sync Level 6** (2026-11-21).
- **No Kodiaks on Dailies**, classic or Draft (2026-11-21). The Gym has none either.
- **Race bears come in packs** (2026-11-21). Whether same-type or mixed packs play better is an
  open playtest question. The Settings switch **KODIAK PACKS: SAME TYPE / MIXED** chooses between
  them, defaulting to SAME TYPE.

## How each type plays

The types differ in mechanics, not only in look.

| | Brute-Force (brown grizzly) | Kernel-Panic (white polar) | Black-Ice (black bear) |
|---|---|---|---|
| Shape | heavy, shoulder hump | long, low body, long neck | round, tall rounded ears, tan muzzle |
| Arrives | from behind, faster than you | ahead, slower, so you close on it | ahead, matching you; never blocks the ground |
| Attack | rears up for 0.65 s, then charges | up to 2 amber feints, then a red telegraph and it stops dead in your line | while you're airborne, commits to your predicted landing (a glint marks it) and slides there |
| Counter | hop the charge; it runs well clear, then slows to 40%, so re-passing is a clean hop | hop it at speed, or it shoves you back | change the landing: divebomb short, or parachute/airbrake long |

**Packs** have 2 or 3 bears; the first pack of a run is always 2.
- **SAME TYPE:** every bear is the same type.
- **MIXED:** every bear differs, so a pack of three has all three types. A mixed pack can pincer,
  with a grizzly behind and the others ahead.

**Contact is a shove, not a crash** (`kodiakKnockback`, `KODIAK_KNOCK`):
- The sled is pushed **backward**, against its travel, and **up**. The backward push is bounded
  (80 px/s base, plus 0.35 × the closing speed, capped at 220 px/s). The lift is 200 px/s.
- Each bear shoves at most once per encounter. After any shove, no bear shoves again for 1 s, so a
  pack cannot chain shoves.
- Shields, Hashed Backup and the respawn grace do not apply: there is no crash to absorb. A sled
  that is invulnerable (respawn grace) is not shoved either.
- A shoved sled keeps going. The shove does not stop it, and the run carries on.
- All three bears share one envelope until playtests say otherwise. The owner has not chosen
  per-bear values.

**Fairness rules**, enforced in `stepKodiakPack` and the unit tests:
- Only one bear telegraphs or attacks at a time.
- No bear starts an attack while another is within 220 px of the sled, close enough to need
  hopping.
- A bear you must pass on the ground is always much slower than you, so a hop clears it at real
  closing speed.
- A bear never slows down underneath a sled that's in the air.

**Scoring and outcomes:**
- Passing a bear on the ground scores +150. You can only pass on the ground after it has stopped
  or tired.
- Clearing a bear in the air (BEAR HOP) scores +250.
- A shoved bear earns no pass bonus. Touching a bear never scores.
- A sled that is shoved can still lose the race by an ordinary route: into terrain, into a chasm,
  or into the BSOD. Those use the existing crash paths, so the crash report names the terrain
  or hazard, never a bear.

**Determinism:**
- Bears advance in the fixed simulation tick, with a tick-exact box collision and no Matter body.
- The schedule, type, pack size, mix and feints all come from seeded streams per encounter.
- Ghost tapes record `kodiaksOn`, `kodiakPackMode` and `kodiakVersion`. A Kodiak run replays
  exactly at 30 and 120 Hz.
- `KODIAK_VERSION` is **3** (v2 was crash-on-contact). The ghost library does not offer a Kodiak
  tape recorded under another version, since it would not replay.

## Evidence

`tools/kodiak-browser-smoke.mjs`, real game, Sync Level 6. It covers three types × SAME/MIXED.
- **Contact shoves.** A rider who does nothing is shoved by every pack with a Kernel-Panic or
  Brute-Force in it. A Black-Ice pack never reaches a rider who stays on the ground.
- **No bear crashes the sled.** No run ends by a bear, and every shove is backward, up, and within
  the bound. No bear shoves twice.
- **A shoved sled keeps racing.** After a shove the run continues for seconds, and a shoved sled
  keeps moving forward.
- **A skilled rider is never crashed by a bear.** In two Brute-Force packs a skilled rider was
  shoved and then fell into a chasm. That is the intended secondary loss, but it is a balance item.
- **No Kodiaks below Sync Level 6.**
- **Replay is exact** at 30 and 120 Hz, with shoves in the tape.

Balance problems found and fixed while proving the earlier crash version:
- A tired grizzly crawled ahead at 80% of the player's speed, too slow a closing speed to hop.
- A grizzly slowed down under a sled that was in the air.
- Black-Ice re-aimed at the landing every tick, so it couldn't be dodged.
- Two bears could attack at once.

## Still open

- Marvin's playtest verdict on SAME TYPE vs MIXED.
- Tuning: the shove strength (`KODIAK_KNOCK`), whether the three bears get different envelopes,
  the encounter spacing (first at 450 m, then every 650–1200 m), the pack sizes, and the telegraph
  timings.
- A shove that drops a skilled rider into a chasm: the Brute-Force shove may be too strong or too
  easy to take on a hop.
- Angular rotation on the shove (optional in the issue) is not implemented. Any trick points come
  from the existing trick rules, not from contact.
- Rival racing behaviour (overtaking, defending a line, competing for the win): the shoves are the
  only contact behaviour so far. The bears still follow the attack scripts above.
- Kodiaks in AlphaBluffs (Idle and the Evolution Lab, the observation schema) once those modes
  exist in this codebase.# Issue #51 — Music-reactive billboards, downbeat scenery, and adaptive minimap

Owner brief: https://github.com/truevox/BitstreamBluffs/issues/51

**Development protocol:** issue first, then draft PR, then implementation and tests. This file is a design-only PR bootstrap, not a completed feature.

The game world should show a *real YouTube thumbnail* billboard once per song change, passing the camera at a screen-visible height without hiding the racer; a gentle spotlight follows it. No redundant toast. A local-only track needs a truthful fallback.

World Dance should combine original OGG mix intensity/transients with small synthetic beat accents and stronger artificial downbeat accents from `AudioSynth.getSyntheticMeterTiming()`. Beat/downbeat boundaries must be edge-triggered on transport revision and index changes. The grid is unverified and cosmetic-only, never authoritative for physics or scoring. On pause/mute/fatal recovery, dancing should cease, without freezing core gameplay.

A small terrain-aware minimap should be top-right when the rider is bottom-left, otherwise bottom-left; it must not conceal other UI or imply globally known offscreen locations. Reduce Motion and World Dance OFF should suppress ornamental movement but not hide useful static information.

Music Friday owns real beat/phase research and YouTube source associations; CC Friday can review UI and title-overhaul overlap. Implement via separate modules; review with real browser smoke; do not merge without checks.

## First implementation — in draft PR #52 (engineering, not a new owner decree)

### Song thumbnail billboards

The three public YouTube-song IDs already present in the **canonical shipped soundtrack catalog** have local bundled 320×180 JPEGs fetched once from YouTube's thumbnail CDN and saved under `public/music/thumbnails/`. The original screenshots are not altered to create AI-imagined artwork:

- `FEsxYVStla4` → `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/FEsxYVStla4/mqdefault.jpg`
- `WRBGQUqD54U` → `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/WRBGQUqD54U/mqdefault.jpg`
- `FYImnz5-hXs` → `https://i.ytimg.com/vi/FYImnz5-hXs/mqdefault.jpg`

`local-one-more-run-bitstream-bluffs` presently has no **catalog-confirmed** YouTube association and therefore gets a truthful local fallback. Music Friday has been asked on issue #51 to confirm any official video association. The source ID belongs to the track, not an arbitrary internet search match. Preload fetches these bundled files; **no YouTube call occurs at runtime or per frame**. A failed thumbnail falls back to the readable sign.

The song-change event comes from the continuously running soundtrack's `onTrackStarted` hook. The game never restarts music just to display a sign. The RunScene billboard is a shallow, decorative layer behind terrain, hazards and the rider: a metal frame, song name, honest cover image and subtle spotlight. It scrolls from the right via a combination of real camera travel and gentle minimum pass speed, and uses the actual terrain silhouette to keep its bottom above the ground so it remains visible. Repeat entry/retry of the exact *same* currently playing song is deduplicated for the lifetime of the shared AudioSynth. The old full-width song-change toast is removed, while unrelated run/achievement/biome notifications remain intact.

### Cosmetic-only beat/downbeat treatment

`WorldDanceEnvelope.update` now accepts an OPTIONAL `AudioSynth.getSyntheticMeterTiming()` snapshot alongside the original, *real* WebAudio mix energy sample. Synthetic beat-index and measure-index changes trigger once per edge; beat 1 of a **new synthetic measure** produces a larger accent. The visible meter component is gated by the actual audible mix energy; no soundtrack means no rhythmic pulses. It cannot touch the validated Gym beat gate, collision, physics, scoring, transport, count-in or recording provenance. Every public song grid remains marked **unverified** and the arbitrary 0.5-second pulse / 4-beat measure is never promoted to real musical timing. Music Friday owns source-aligned beat/downbeat research.

### Rider-avoiding minimap

A tiny 104×49px drawing in UIScene samples the same deterministic `TerrainGenerator.heightAt` as the physical world, roughly 150px behind and 550px ahead. It plots terrain shape and a rider marker; it does **not** claim hazard discovery or hidden offscreen items. Its screen-space layout flips to top-right whenever the player occupies the bottom-left quadrant, otherwise bottom-left, leaving the centre of the race unobstructed. It is read-only, uses only ~27 samples at approximately 9Hz, and does not update physical world state.

### Accessibility and limitations

The World Dance ON/OFF and Reduce Motion Auto/On/Off settings already shipped in PR #38 govern the rhythmic animation, including the billboard squash. Under silence, manual pause, fatal-count-in or muted music the mixer and synthetic meter return null and the dance envelope resets. The billboard and minimap are informative static objects even with reduced-motion preference. No new controls or changes to the title-screen overhaul are part of the implementation.

**Honest prototype boundaries:** The billboard is decorative rather than a physically collidable sign; its scroll speed is not a literal vehicle-world-speed conversion. The local song's fallback is intentionally not claimed to be YouTube art. The minimap is local terrain only, not a gameplay radar or world map. The synthetic downbeat is a provisional visual cue, not a recognized beat in the recording. Gameplay balance/physics remain unchanged.# Issue #54: catalog-driven billboard artwork + PR #52 HUD review fixes

Child PR of draft PR #52 (`feature/song-billboards-meter-minimap-51`). Opened
before implementation, per the issue → branch → draft PR workflow. Owner: CC Friday.

