- `tools/hud-minimap-touch-browser-smoke.mjs`: tablet and phone touch viewports;
  the minimap never overlaps the touch buttons.# Issue #56: ghost library and replay tapes (spec draft)

Status: **slice 1 implemented** on `feature/ghost-tapes-56`, after Marvin authorized ghost
work on 2026-11-20:
- tapes are recorded for normal and Daily runs (never Gym);
- the library keeps Latest and Best per course, plus Favorites in the data model;
- ghosts are drawn visual-only from the recorded trace;
- Settings has BEST GHOST and LATEST GHOST switches.

Not built yet:
- the Favorites UI;
- archive capacity, because its economics are undecided;
- the off-screen re-simulation of tapes that arrive without a trace (needed for #58 imports).

The tape format is still v1 and provisional. Items marked **[Marvin]** are open owner
decisions.

**Proof:** `tools/ghost-tape-browser-smoke.mjs`, real game, three seeds, runs of 6–10 s.
- Recording a run saves a 4–7 KB tape.
- Replaying it from the tape alone matches every checkpoint exactly at 30 Hz and at 120 Hz,
  and crashes on the same tick.
- The next run on the course draws one BEST+LAST ghost.
- With that ghost on screen, the same live inputs reproduce identical physics checkpoints.

## Owner decisions this spec follows (2026-11-20)

- A ghost belongs to an exact **course**: the seed plus the selected biome mask. Ghosts never
  race across seeds.
- A course shows **at most five** ghosts: Latest, Best, and Favorites A, B and C. Each one can
  be toggled separately. A run that holds several roles is drawn once.
- Ghosts have no collision, scoring, hazard, Daily-ranking or award effects.
- The repeatable **Ghost Library / Course Archive** upgrade raises how many *courses* keep their
  ghosts. It never raises the five-per-course limit.
- Replay works from the initial state plus a compact tick-indexed input tape, with sparse
  checkpoints to detect desync.
- Ghosts are local and offline by default. Publishing to the Brain Bazaar happens only when the
  player explicitly asks (#58).

## Data model

```
CourseKey   = "<seed>|<biomeMask as 8 hex digits>|gen<generatorVersion>"
CourseEntry = { key, seed, biomeMask, generatorVersion,
                latest: runId|null, best: runId|null,
                favorites: [runId|null, runId|null, runId|null],
                show: { latest, best, fav0, fav1, fav2 } (booleans, default true),
                lastPlayedAt, bytes }
GhostTape   = stored once per runId, referenced from any number of roles
```

- **Best** is the furthest distance on that course. Ties go to the earlier run. Gym practice
  attempts don't count (this is the proposed default) **[Marvin]**.
- **Latest** is the most recent finished run on the course.
- A tape is deleted only when no role on any course references it any more.

## Replay tape v0 (provisional)

**Header:**
- `format: 'bb-ghost-tape'`, `version`;
- `simTickHz`, `physicsVersion`, `generatorVersion`, `runRandomVersion`, `actionSchemaVersion`;
- `seed`, `biomeMask`, `mode` (normal, Daily or Gym) plus the Daily rule ID;
- the frozen loadout (boosts, upgrades and cosmetics that affect physics);
- the initial pose and velocity;
- the `runRandomState()` snapshot and the attempt-start tick (Gym retries continue the tick
  counter);
- `runId` and `recordedAt`.

**Inputs:** one entry per tick on which the normalized pad changes, recorded as `[tickDelta,
changedFieldMask, values]`, run-length encoded and stored as base64 bytes:
- held fields: push, tilt (−1/0/1), tuck, brake, divebomb, corkscrew, parachute and airBrake;
- one-shot edges, recorded on the tick that consumed them: hop, reset, parachute and airBrake.
  Pause is never recorded, because the run clock doesn't advance during it.

**Checkpoints:** about once a second (every 120 ticks). Each holds the tick, x, y, angle, vx,
vy, angular velocity and score as float32, plus a 32-bit checksum.

**Size:** a 3-minute run has about 1,000 input changes (≈4 KB) and 180 checkpoints (≈6 KB), so
roughly 10 KB per tape. One full course is about 50 KB, and 200 courses about 10 MB. Tapes
belong in IndexedDB (the `runs` store already lives there), not localStorage.

**Playback:**
1. Re-simulate the tape once, off-screen, on a hidden Matter world when the course loads.
2. Verify each checkpoint and cache the resulting pose trace.
3. Draw up to five ghosts from their traces each frame, without running five live physics
   simulations.

On a checkpoint mismatch or a version mismatch, the tape is labelled **incompatible** and is
not drawn. The game never invents a path. Verified pose traces may be kept as a fallback for
older engine versions **[Marvin]**.

**Separation:**
- Tapes live in their own store, keyed by `runId`.
- `RunRecord` and the run-ledger hashes don't change.
- Ghosts are drawn by a visual-only entity outside `simTick`, with no Matter bodies and no
  event emission.

## Archive capacity

The number of courses kept is `includedCourses + purchasedSlots`. Several values are still
open **[Marvin]**:
- the starting count;
- the price curve;
- the currency.

The proposed eviction rules:
- When a run would create a new course entry and the archive is full, the
  least-recently-played course *without favorites* is replaced, after a one-line notice.
- If every course has favorites, the player is asked to manage the archive. Favorites are
  never deleted silently.
- Latest and Best on the current course always update in place.

## UI (proposal)

- A per-course ghost panel on the pre-run screen: five rows (Latest, Best, A, B, C), each with
  an on/off switch, the run's distance and its date, and *Save as favorite* / *Replace* on the
  results screen.
- A global *Hide ghosts* switch exists only as a convenience over the per-ghost switches.
- Ghosts are translucent, tinted by role, with no trails that could be read as hazards. They
  honour reduced motion.

## Test plan (when implementation is authorized)

1. **Same build, same tape:** the replay matches the recorded checkpoints, at 30, 60 and
   120 Hz, with scramble, slow-mo and hit-stop, through a crash and a Gym retry. The base for
   this is `tools/replay-determinism-browser-probe.mjs`.
2. **Desync detection:** a corrupted tape, a mismatched version, or a tape for the wrong course
   is rejected and not drawn.
3. **Library rules:**
   - the five-per-course limit, with duplicate roles drawn once;
   - favorite pin, unpin and replace;
   - Latest and Best updates;
   - the archive-full flow that never deletes favorites;
   - quota exhaustion in IndexedDB.
4. **Gameplay isolation:** with ghosts visible, a run's physics checkpoints, score and
   `RunRecord` hash are identical to the same tape with ghosts hidden.
5. **Privacy:** no network request happens on save, favorite or slot purchase. Publishing
   (#58) is a separate, explicit action.

## Order of work

#59 stage 2 is merged and checked by hand →
tape recorder plus off-screen re-sim (behind a flag) →
library store and UI →
archive capacity, once the economics are decided →
#58 publishing.# Issue #59: fixed simulation tick and seeded run randomness

Status: **stages 1 and 2 merged to `main` on 2026-11-20 at Marvin's request.** Marvin chose
the 120 Hz tick (option A below). His hands-on play session is still pending, and the gates
listed at the end remain open.

| Stage | Branch | What it changes |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | `engine/seeded-sim-rng-59` | Scramble torque comes from a seeded `physics/scramble` stream. The scrambled-silhouette shake uses `visual/sled`. New run seeds come from `crypto.getRandomValues`. |
| 2 | `engine/fixed-tick-59` (on top of 1) | All causal gameplay runs in fixed ticks (`SimClock`, `RunScene.simTick`). |
| 3 | `feature/ghost-tapes-56` | Done. No `Math.random()` is left in `src/`, and a test enforces it. World effects (`Fx`, camera shake) use the run's `visual/fx` and `visual/camera` streams, so a replay looks the same. Menus, HUD flicker, the jukebox, audio texture and music shuffle use `CosmeticRandom`: named forks of a session root taken once from the CSPRNG. Not yet audited: `tools/` and future simulation code outside `src/`. |

## What stage 2 does

- `RunScene.update` keeps per-display-frame work: the pause check, the crash and Gym READY
  holds, and `renderFrame`. Everything that was below the pause check now lives in
  `simTick(dt, pad)`, unchanged except that `dt` is always the tick:
  - timers, cooldowns, slow-mo, blink and hit-stop;
  - Matter steps, contacts and the BSOD chase and catch;
  - tricks, score and flow, pickups and hazards;
  - terrain streaming and the glitch schedule.
- `SimClock` (pure, unit-tested):
  - It accumulates real frame time and runs whole ticks only.
  - A frame runs at most 6 ticks (50 ms, the old frame clamp). Any backlog beyond that is
    dropped, so a stalled tab never fast-forwards the run.
  - Held controls come from the latest poll. A one-shot `pressed` edge reaches exactly one
    tick. If a frame runs no tick, the edge waits for the next tick rather than being lost.
  - `simClock.tick` is an integer tick counter, which is the time axis a replay tape needs.
- Slow-mo still scales the physics delta inside each tick, so it remains deterministic.

## Evidence (CT223, Chromium + SwiftShader, minified build, real Matter)

`tools/replay-determinism-browser-probe.mjs` gives each run its own browser page. It holds
`RunScene.update`, puts Phaser's loop to sleep, and steps `game.step(t, dt)` on an exact
frame schedule with a scripted input tape (push, hops, flips, tuck). Scramble is forced on
for the whole run.

| Check | Variable delta (`main`) | Stage 1 | Stage 1 + 2 |
|---|---|---|---|
| Same seed, tape and 60 Hz schedule, twice | **diverges on frame 1** (41 px, score 199 vs 201) | identical | identical |
| 60 Hz vs 30 Hz, 9 s | 1,315 px apart | 1,315 px apart | **0 px** (same pose, velocity, angle and score at every sample) |
| 60 Hz vs 120 Hz, 9 s | 130 px apart | 130 px apart | **0 px** |

### Handling compared with today's game (scramble off, 3 seeds, 12 s, no-flip tape)

- **Against today's 120 Hz:** stage 2 matches within about 1 px, with the same scores and crash
  times. This is by design, because every tick is two 4.17 ms Matter steps, exactly what a
  120 Hz display runs today.
- **Against today's 60 Hz:** distance differs by 14–26 px over 4,000–6,000 px (about 0.5%).
  Crashes fall in the same places, about 25–70 ms apart. Two of three scores match; the third
  is 317 vs 344.
- **With a flip in the tape:** a borderline 3 s flip lands at 60 Hz today but bails at 30 and
  120 Hz, in both the old and the new code. The trajectories then diverge by thousands of px.
  Today's outcome for that run depends on the display rate.

**Finding:** today a run's result depends on the player's monitor. At the same seed and
inputs, 30 Hz and 60 Hz players ended 3,709 px apart (scores 186 vs 386). That is an official
Daily fairness problem on `main`, separate from ghosts.

## Decision for Marvin: tick rate

| Option | Physics per tick | 30/60/120 Hz smoothness | Handling vs today | Cost |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| **A. 120 Hz (8.33 ms), the branch default** | 2 × 4.17 ms | exact (4/2/1 ticks per frame) | identical to today's 120 Hz; about 0.5% off today's 60 Hz | 2 ticks per 60 Hz frame |
| B. 180 Hz (5.56 ms) | 1 × 5.56 ms | 30/60 exact, 120 Hz judders | identical to today's 60 Hz | 3 ticks per 60 Hz frame, and 120 Hz needs render interpolation |
| C. 125 Hz (8 ms, the issue's candidate) | 1 × 8 ms | judders at 30/60/120 without interpolation | new step size, untested | needs render interpolation |

**Recommendation:** A. It reuses a step size players already have, it needs no interpolation at
common refresh rates, and its parity is proven above. On 144/165 Hz displays some frames run
no tick and the motion steps slightly. Bounded render interpolation is a follow-up if that
shows in play.

## Replay hardening (2026-11-20)

`tools/ghost-tape-browser-smoke.mjs` stage 6 records one run that includes a manual reset, a real
pause and resume through the Pause screen, and a 2-second stalled frame (a background tab coming
back). It then replays the tape alone at 30, 60 and 120 Hz. All 12 checkpoints match exactly, and
every replay crashes on the same tick.

Three frame-rate leaks were found and fixed on the way:

1. **Terrain chunks became physical on frame-dependent ticks.** Cosmetic code (the minimap's
   look-ahead, the billboard's posts) calls `terrain.heightAt` once per display frame. That can make
   the generator build chunks early, and their colliders, hazards and pickups then joined the world
   on whatever tick came next, which slightly reordered Matter's contact solving. Now
   `RunScene.streamTerrain` adds a chunk only on the first tick the sled is within the generator's
   own fill-ahead bound of it.
2. **Rolling boulders and gates moved their sensor bodies per display frame.** `Hazard.update`
   ran during rendering. Motion now advances once per tick in `simTick`.
3. **The helicopter-spin angle, which the trick tracker scores, advanced in `Sled.render`.** It is
   now `Sled.stepHeli`, called once per tick.

Replays are also now driven by the tape header alone (seed, biome mask, upgrade ranks, boosts,
`magnetsDrop`), not the live profile, and they never save a run record, award progress or record
a new tape.

Parity is also proven on a biome course with a rolling boulder active: `SEED=RPLAY59@260
START_X=50400`, with helicopter input in the tape, is exact at 30/60/120 Hz.

## Still open

- A hands-on feel check by Marvin.
- Gym in-scene retries inside one tape (Gym runs record no tapes yet), and long runs over
  60 seconds.
- Cross-browser and cross-version parity. Same-build parity is not cross-version parity.
- Official run-ledger hashes are untouched. Tapes have their own versioned format (#56).# #70: Offline Daily course shortlist (developer tool)

Use the existing #126 harness to record one small, comparable panel. Run it
from the game and harness revision you intend to identify. For example:

```sh
node tools/sim/run.mjs --noiseProfiles ideal,steady,distracted \
  --seeds sim-01,sim-02,sim-03 --biomeMask 0 --rule none \
  --maxM 500 --maxSec 25 --verify --out bot-panel.json

node tools/sim/shortlist-daily.mjs --panel bot-panel.json \
  --build "$PRODUCER_COMMIT" > shortlist.json
```

`PRODUCER_COMMIT` is the full 40-hex Git commit for the game and harness
checkout that actually produced the panel, not necessarily the revision of
this reader. If the producer had local changes, do not present its commit as
an exact source pin. The flag is a **caller assertion**: this reader does not
resolve Git, inspect the producing checkout, or authenticate the file. It
prints the SHA-256 of the exact input bytes to identify which file it read.
Given those same bytes and the same commit, it writes byte-identical JSON.

The reader runs offline and opens no browser, network connection, or save.
It takes one regular UTF-8 JSON file up to 4 MiB, and writes only to stdout
(or a destination chosen through shell redirection). It requires the
successful classic (noise model v1) #126 `--verify` report, ideal plus at
least one noisy profile, 1–12 distinct seeds, and exactly one result per
seed/profile pair under the panel's settings. It checks the two runner-reported verification flags in
every cell, the profile summaries, and basic structure and consistency of
the original trial's input encoding, frame/tick counts, pose samples, planner
counters and terminal fields. Failed checks exit nonzero rather than counting as
failed runs. This does not check all policy constraints or recalculate
physics from the input. The report does **not** carry the alternate
repeat/playback runs, so the reader cannot recompute those comparisons
without running the harness again. The hash identifies source bytes; it does
not prove who wrote them.

The runner's optional `--fatigue` mode produces a separate noise-model-v2
panel, which this shortlist does not interpret. Even if that panel verified
successfully, generate a classic panel with `--verify` and without
`--fatigue` for this reader. Do not relabel or combine the two panel formats.

## Reading the shortlist

A candidate is a seed where `ideal` reached the distance threshold and at
least one tested noisy profile did not. Crashes, simulated timeouts and
stalls remain distinct in the evidence. A seed that only a noisy profile
passed is not shortlisted without a passing ideal baseline. Unselected
seeds remain visible as controls. Seeds are listed in code-unit order, not
ranked by score, distance, or inferred difficulty. The output contains the
seed/settings, per-profile observations, and the report digest and build
assertion. It does not export score, input rows, poses, replay data, or
raw terminal state.

The profiles are **variants of one synthetic hop policy**, not different
player populations or independent riding policies. Threshold contrast is a
cue for playtesting, not evidence of fairness. At 500 m and mask 0 this
experiment does not compare biomes, detect geometric diversity, generate a
candidate pool, select an official Daily, or change Daily rules or standings.# #71: local replay input constraints (developer pilot)

This tool checks three explicit candidate constraints on **the recorded input of one saved normal replay**. It does not create a challenge in the game, verify a player or course, set a distance/survival goal, modify saves, or transfer any result to Daily or ranked play. Issue #71 remains open for player-facing design and broader acceptance.

```sh
npm run build
npm run preview -- --host 127.0.0.1 --port 8293
# In another terminal; use the full 40-hex commit you built.
node tools/sim/verify-input-constraints.mjs --build FULL_SAME_BUILD_SHA \
  --url http://127.0.0.1:8293/ --tape /path/to/local-normal-run.json
# Optional: add --max-hop-edges 1 to use a stricter budget.
```

Use your trusted, same-build compiled preview and an already available local GhostTape JSON. `CHROMIUM_EXECUTABLE` can select a compatible local Chromium. The full SHA is a caller assertion, not verified build provenance. This does not authenticate the tape, extract it from a profile, or promise compatibility across builds or browsers. It has the same strict current normal v1 intake as the crash brief (2 MiB; up to 180 s of completed ticks; known starting options; at least one and all correctly shaped on-cadence checkpoints).

The tool reads the tape once and feeds that same data to two fresh disposable Chromium contexts using the existing crash verifier. Each run must reconstruct the initial options, exactly match every recorded completed checkpoint, and naturally crash on `endTick + 1`; both runs must have identical observed state and fatal event and save no run or ghost. In a separate pass over the same tape, the production `TapePlayer` reconstructs each normalized tick pad from tick 1 through that reproduced fatal tick, inclusive. The recorder captures the pad before simulating the tick, even when that last tick produces no completed checkpoint.

| Candidate ID (v1) | How it is satisfied |
| --- | --- |
| `no-hop-request` | Zero ticks where normalized hop was held **or** `pressed.hop` was true. |
| `no-parachute-request` | Zero ticks where normalized parachute was held **or** `pressed.parachute` was true. |
| `hop-edge-budget` | No more than `--max-hop-edges` ticks with a true normalized `pressed.hop` edge. Inclusive, default 2, integer 0–120. A held bit alone is not a press. |

A held bit persists through sparse rows until changed; an encoded press applies only on its row tick. A held control and its press on the same tick count as one requested tick. These are recorded **requests**, even if the sled could not hop or deploy the canopy. They are not physical action, keyboard tap, technique diversity, or button-event counts outside the recorded fixed-tick interval. These rules intentionally ignore other controls and layout configuration flags.

Only after successful replay does stdout contain aggregate input counts and candidate definitions with per-rule `met` true/false. A valid replay with an unmet rule still exits successfully; malformed, unsupported, or nonreproducing input exits nonzero with no receipt. The report omits the tape bytes, local path, URL, seed, identity, raw build SHA, exact duration, event/cause, and scores. Counts can still help distinguish cases; this is not an anonymous attestation. The trusted loopback game receives the raw tape transiently in the disposable contexts. Only same-origin GET/HEAD are allowed; redirects, WebSockets, and service workers are blocked. The tool neither writes a report file nor sends it anywhere.

Focused checks: `npm test -- --runInBand tests/src/input-constraints.test.js tests/src/black-box-crash-brief.test.js tests/src/replay-airtime.test.js`.# #77: a fixed stock neutral-run panel

