Inspect an existing **local GhostTape v1 JSON file** without starting the game
or replaying its inputs. This is a standalone developer tool. The game does
not yet supply a player-facing tape JSON export or an in-game strip button.

1. From the checkout: `npm run dev -- --host 127.0.0.1`.
2. Open `http://127.0.0.1:5173/tools/downhill-run-strip.html` (use the port
   printed by Vite if different). Choose the local JSON file.
3. Open an evenly spaced section, enter a first and last sample, or zoom
   around the current sample. Move the slider with the arrow keys to inspect
   exact stored X and Y. **Show full trace** returns to the overview.
4. Use **Download section SVG** to save the displayed interval. The SVG can
   be viewed and shared without the inspector. It is static and does not
   include the preview cursor or a way to reopen the source tape.
5. **Run DNA preview** shows local, unverified artwork from the full accepted
   path. Zooming never changes this artwork or the strip export. The preview
   is not exported by the inspector, and choosing or clearing a file removes
   the old artwork immediately. See [the Run DNA notes](issue-87-run-trace-art.md).

The plotted line joins recorded X/Y samples in their original order, including
backtracking. It fits the chosen section with the **same unit scale on both
axes**, +X right and +Y down. These are game-world coordinates, not elevation
or measured distance. The inspector reads exact decoded Float32 coordinates;
the SVG contains only projected path geometry, not the world origin. Window
endpoints and the actual first and last **recorded samples** are labeled
separately. The first recorded sample is at tick 4; v1 saves one pose every 4
ticks at nominal 120 Hz. This does not identify the exact run start or crash.

To bound large overviews, windows above 1,024 poses draw the endpoints and the
recorded X/Y extrema of small consecutive buckets, in original order. The live
preview also retains the selected pose so its ring always sits on the path.
A static export does not include this selection-only vertex. The image
says how many vertices it shows; zoom into at most 1,024 poses to see each
recorded pose. There is no smoothing or extrapolation. The inspector accepts a
UTF-8 JSON file up to 2 MiB with 2–32,768 complete v1 pose triples (Float32 X,
Y and angle); exact pose encoding and finite bounded components are checked.
Other tape data and verification claims are not used. SVG output is capped at
48 KiB and is byte-identical for the same decoded poses and selected interval
in the same tool version. Display fonts can differ across viewers.

**Local / unverified** means the inspector does not verify the replay or certify
play. The file is read in browser memory without uploads, saved history or app
state changes. The downloaded SVG has no seed, run or account ID, score, date,
inputs, tape payload, scripts, external links or assets. The depicted path and
sample range can still identify a recognizable run; this is not anonymization.

Terrain, biomes, tricks, BSOD proximity, hazards and crash markers are not in
this stored trace and are not guessed. Those annotations and player-facing
integration remain follow-up work; scoring, saves and replay contracts are
unchanged.# #81: local ghost lineup on Game Over

Open **GHOST FAV** on Game Over (or press **G**) after a run that saved a
local ghost tape. **Course Ghosts** shows the completed run's seed and biome
mask's five existing roles: **Best** (furthest), **Latest**, and **Favorites
A–C**. Changes apply the next time you enter that exact course; opening,
changing or backing out of the panel never starts a run.

- Use Up/Down to select a row and Left/Right to switch that role on or off.
  Enter switches Best/Latest, or saves/replaces/clears the run in a Favorite.
  The panel still opens on Favorite A, so G then Enter still pins this run.
  The corresponding buttons also work with pointer/touch.
- **Show** is a course preference. Existing global Best/Latest choices in
  Settings still win over that preference. **Off in Settings** explains
  when a role cannot draw even if its course switch is on.
- A role can be **Empty**, have a **Tape Missing**, or be **Incompatible**
  with the current ghost renderer, or have **No Trace** if the run ended
  before a pose was recorded. Its preference can still apply to a later
  available tape. **Shared Run** means another role has the same
  compatible tape: it draws only once if any of its roles can show, and
  turning one role off will not hide a tape still selected through another.

The panel uses the existing format/physics/tick checks and the same current
simulation-revision check as the in-run ghost display. A tape with a different
or missing revision shows **Incompatible** and won't draw on the current
terrain. It remains in its slot until replaced or cleared. The panel flags
empty traces but does not decode or validate the pose data. These are
local cosmetic traces, not proof of identical rules or loadout, competitive
parity, or human play. There are no friends, uploads, imports, score/standings changes, replay
changes, or new settings or storage contracts in this slice.# #82: Local percentile and Pareto calculations

This is a calculation-only slice. It does not change what the game saves,
scores, displays, or ranks. There is no production call site or new standings
view.

```js
import { calculateLocalRankings } from './src/systems/local-rankings.js';

const history = await saveStore.getRecentRuns(500);
const analysis = calculateLocalRankings(history, {
  board: 'clean',
  seed: 'ALPHA',
  biomeMask: 0,
  dailyRuleId: 'featherweight',
});
```

The caller picks a finite recent-history window explicitly: the example
uses the 500 most recent stored records **before** eligibility and cohort
filtering. This is not an all-time or cross-player sample. The calculation
rejects inputs above 1,000 records, rather than silently trimming them.
It does not read IndexedDB itself or mutate the supplied history.

## Cohorts and valid input

Use the existing public SaveStore history method. Unlike exported standings,
this method retains the local `imported` marker. The calculation includes only
records with initials, a completed crash (not a quit), no local import marker,
and a matching ledger hash under supported v2, v3, or v4. It rejects unversioned
v1 and unknown ledger versions even where the old verifier's fallback succeeds:
v1 does not bind the fields needed to identify this cohort. Other leaderboard
surfaces retain their original behavior.

Select one board, exact stored seed, unsigned biome mask, movement-rule ID,
and exact known applied loadout. An omitted recorded mask means 0. An omitted
selection boost list or upgrade snapshot means empty, not “any.” Known boosts
are compared as an order-independent set; upgrade ranks are the known positive
applied ranks, not the entire profile. V2 cannot attest to applied upgrade
ranks. Hash-consistent rows with an impossible board/loadout combination do
not enter a cohort.

- `clean`: ordinary and typed-seed practice runs accepted by the current
  clean eligibility helper, with no applied boosts or ranks in this slice.
- `modded`: ordinary runs accepted by the current modded eligibility helper,
  with a known nonempty loadout. Different boost sets or upgrade ranks never mix.
- `daily-classic`: official Classic runs accepted by the current Daily helper,
  with no applied boosts or ranks in this slice.
- `daily-draft`: runs accepted by the current official Draft helper, including
  its current rules version and budget check. Select the exact applied Draft
  ranks. A correctly stamped but unrankable official run, or an invalid
  official stamp, does not fall back to ordinary or modded analytics.

Malformed, unsupported, or hash-mismatched rows are skipped, not repaired.
Analysis-equivalent copies of the same verified run ID count once; if two
eligible copies conflict on hash, cohort, or metric values, exclude the ID
from every cohort. Run IDs order output deterministically using string order.
That order is not a claim about official placement. Initials and imported
provenance are local metadata, not fields vouched for by the ledger hash.

## Metrics and statistics

All objectives are higher-is-better. Calculations use full numeric values,
without rounding for ties or comparisons:

| Key | Units | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `distanceM` | m | Stored furthest distance |
| `score` | points | Stored final score, unchanged |
| `averageProgressMps` | m/s | Furthest metres divided by active run seconds; an average progress rate, not measured instantaneous or peak speed |
| `trickVariety` | types | Count of stored trick types with a positive count |
| `efficiencyPointsPerM` | points/m | Final score divided by furthest metres |
| `maxAirtimeMs` | ms | Stored longest airtime |
| `survivalMs` | ms | Stored active run duration until a crash, excluding pauses; not wall-clock time and not a count of quits |

Zero or unavailable duration makes average progress and survival unavailable.
Zero distance makes points per metre unavailable. Nonfinite derived values are
also unavailable. An unavailable metric is `null`, not a minimum score; its
record is still eligible for other metrics. Missing or invalid required base
measurements in the supplied row cause exclusion. Previously normalized stored
defaults cannot generally be distinguished from actual recorded zeroes.

The per-metric sample size counts available values. Fewer than two means
**no percentile** (`null`). Otherwise the percentile is
`100 × (strictly lower count + equal count / 2) / available count`.
Equal values share a percentile; the best need not be 100. Percentiles
describe runs within this sample, not unique people.

A run dominates another if it is at least as good on **every** metric and
better on at least one. Identical vectors never dominate each other. Only
complete vectors participate; incomplete vectors are unassessed (`pareto:
null`), not ranked last. A one-run complete sample is trivially nondominated,
not evidence of superiority. The report includes both the total cohort size
and the number of complete vectors so sparse metrics stay visible. Pairwise
frontier evaluation is bounded by the explicit history cap.

The ledger verifier checks local record consistency. It is not a cryptographic
signature or independent replay verification. The record has no verified human,
AI, or tool-assisted actor classification, and it does not identify the exact
physics build or capture every gameplay setting. The saved record's schema
version isn't a gameplay ruleset version. Matching stored identifiers
does not prove cross-release fairness. Do not present the result as a public,
verified, or official leaderboard.# #87: Run DNA, local trace artwork

Generate a standalone neon SVG from a **local GhostTape v1 JSON file** you
already have. This developer tool does not add an in-game export button or a way
to retrieve or share tapes.

```sh
node tools/run-trace-art.mjs /path/to/local-ghost-tape.json > run-dna.svg
```

By default the command renders the original Art V1. It reads one regular
file and, on successful conversion, writes only SVG to stdout; it does not
save anything itself, contact a server, or open a browser. Open the SVG
locally to preview it. Keep the raw tape private. The SVG carries the visible
label **LOCAL / UNVERIFIED** and **ART V1**. It is artwork, not a run receipt,
proof of human play, or an assertion that a replay can be verified.

The CLI accepts the current v1 pose representation of three Float32 values
(X, Y, angle) per sample. It requires whole canonical base64 pose blocks and
uses the existing ghost decoder. Header format and version select the byte
layout, not authenticity. JSON is limited to 2 MiB and the trace must have
2–32,768 complete poses. Every component must be finite and within ±1 billion.
Unsupported, malformed, missing, and oversized inputs fail with a nonzero
status and no SVG. The artwork is limited to 8 KiB.

In developer code, the browser-safe `renderRunTraceArt(decodedPoses)` takes
only the decoded Float32 array. V1 removes each axis's original position and
scale, resamples the path to 64 points, and projects it onto a coarse canvas
grid of four-unit steps. Constant axes are centered. V1 validates recorded
angles but does not use them for the motif. Identical decoded poses and art
version produce identical SVG bytes,
although font appearance can differ by platform. Different poses can
produce the same coarse motif. The path may still be recognizable; this
is **not anonymization**.

The artwork carries no account details, run ID, initials, seed, dates,
scores, inputs, checkpoints, replay payload, exact world coordinates,
link, scripts, embeds or external assets. Verification status claims in
the input are ignored. No game screens, scoring, storage, replay formats,
or standings change. Trick cadence, risk, biome events, authenticated run
linkage and sharing are deferred; they are not inferred from the pose path.


## Opt-in Art V2 orientation study

```sh
node tools/run-trace-art.mjs --art-v2 /path/to/local-ghost-tape.json > run-dna-v2.svg
```

The option changes the **art** version, not the input tape version. It uses
the same GhostTape v1 bounds and the same 64-point path as V1, with one fixed
row of 16 accents. Each accent shows the highest of four coarse tiers for
the absolute shortest orientation change between adjacent recorded poses in
that relative band. Empty bands and changes below the first threshold look
the same. The row is not a time axis; it does not identify direction, full spins,
tricks, biome or risk, and can miss motion between recorded poses.

V2 does not alter the default CLI SVG or the developer inspector, which
still uses Art V1. Both outputs remain **LOCAL / UNVERIFIED**. The new motif
may be recognizable and remains artwork, not proof or anonymization.# Plan: Synthwave Arcade-Cabinet overhaul of all menu screens

