## v2.20.0 — owner-approved 60% one-shot AirBrake (SIM_REVISION 9, #247) (2026-11-24)

- **Marvin's direct approval after playtest:** the sharper AirBrake was a huge improvement; the tested **60% instantaneous airborne horizontal speed reduction** with the **same vertical pop** is the approved production behavior, not a provisional feel experiment. Original direct owner affirmation: “I absolutely authorize that. It was a huge improvement over the original.”
- **Exactly one kick per real airtime:** the brake spends horizontal velocity once, triggers the original-speed-budget upward impulse, and then gravity/landing naturally resumes. No held AirBrake float, continuing lift or falling-speed suppression; parachute remains the held airborne control. Existing AirBrake upgrades scale the already-tested upward impulse, not extra hidden ascent from stronger braking.
- **AirBrake interaction and scoring correctness:** A coyote/variable-Hop release cannot cancel a paid AirBrake ascent whether the Hop is triggered in the same physics substep or later inside the coyote window. A new `airBrakeLiftProtected` causal flag persists until real terrain contact, while separate `_protectedHopExtraVyPxS` preserves normal Hop-release shortening of only Hop-added velocity (never the paid brake ascent). This separation works with Hop before or after AirBrake, and holding a spent button no longer bypasses normal crest smoothing. Hop release never adds downward momentum when gravity has already dissipated the Hop ascent and never pauses a sled that is already descending. Hop-added velocity is also damped by Matter `frictionAir` during every physics substep so its later release cannot remove more ascent than remains. Substep-order physical touchdown snapshots apply landing physics before any buffered Hop relaunch; landing awards commit only after the entire fixed tick survives. The regular AirBrake trick credits each physically paid quick tap immediately, including successive short touchdown-only flights and kicks paid on an earlier tick before a brief landing, which bank existing provisional points at contact with bounded actual airtime, no double credit, no fabricated perfect-landing bonus even after Stabilizer forgiveness and no second speed/spring boost, not 250ms of button holding; near-wall 40-point bonus also requires a new physical one-shot kick, not merely a held button. Final independent-review safety corrections re-grade each buffered contact against the slope at its true position, feed genuine mid-tick touchdowns to the BSOD Bluff tracker before a relaunch so no canopy/kick credit crosses flights, scale protected Hop-release velocity with hazard multipliers, and retain normal sloppy-landing wobble even on a tiny paid AirBrake contact. A quick AirBrake press-and-release within one fixed tick pays its legitimate first-substep impulse even when the held state is false. Physical touchdown rebound/wobble and boost apply inside the Matter after-step callback. An ordered tick-local ledger defers points, trick counts, and reward events until terminal collision priority is known; Flow clock steps are replayed in contact order. The AirBrake single-use latch is spent ONLY when real horizontal velocity was paid: vacuum attempts cost nothing and can be retried after entering atmosphere. A purchased upward brake kick resets the existing launch clock to protect crest-adjacent ascent; provisional bad landings cannot mask later fatal sensor hits, and shield-absorbed hazard samples do not reset Bluff encounters. The Flow timer advances before each real Matter substep landing, and the final post-contact flight starts at the last physical touchdown timestamp rather than inheriting the whole fixed tick. The reduced-order BSOD escape envelope uses an immediate `airBrakeKickStep` and normal gravity, replacing its invalid sustained-brake model.
- **Deterministic release SIM 9:** `sled.flags.airBrakeKickUsed` is causal and fingerprinted under sled schema v3. SIM8 cache-pure terrain marker correction and SIM7 owner-approved Fast Approach / Soft Finish BSOD wall remain intact. Historical archives/fixtures are retained as pre-release provenance, not silently rewritten or promised compatible at runtime.
- **Subtle startup version (#315):** native-size pixel text at 65% opacity in the top-left; retains the three-second hold and fade from v2.19.24.
